{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md && cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md && cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json'","aggregated_output":"# 判断的尺子，第 1 版（D3 立场金标）\n\n写于 2026-11-02。Alex 的话：\"你已经改了好几次尺子了，到底什么是对的，以及你怎么 freeze。\"\n\n## 0. 一句话\n\n一道题判得对不对，拿它和**一位资深对冲基金分析师读完同样 14 天材料后的看法**比：他认为市场原来预期什么、材料推出来的结果是多少、\n相对预期是高是低；系统最后一次判断落的那一档，有没有把这个结果包进去。分析师不是我，不是写代码的人，不看系统的输出；他的看法写成文件、算出\nsha256、记进决定，之后一个字不改。\n\n## 1. 什么是\"对\"\n\n- **单位**：一道金标题 × 一个时点（14 天窗口末，2026-07-14 收盘后）。23 道题：22 道种子（`poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json` v1–v22，每次运行里\n  编号固定）加金标 must_open 里种子没覆盖的 1 道（俄亥俄租约担保是/否，2026-07-01 的 must_open）。其余 27 条 must_open 都是这 23 道的同一赌注\n  （信用支持金额 ×10、Kyber 出货 ×11、云厂商资本开支 ×5、内存价格 ×1、中国放行 H200 ×2），映射写在 `questions-v1.json` 里，Alex 签。\n- **材料**：签过的抽取标准对这 14 天的读法，即金标 golden-v5（dev，118 篇）的 309 条事项（核心 86、辅助 151、可选 72），带日期、标题、\n  来源层、说话人、原文和意思。不用系统的账本（账本随每臂变，尺子不能随臂变），不用原始全文（那是重做抽取）。这和抽取的尺子是同一把：标准第 5 节\n  说的\"分析员会记的\"就是这些。\n- **分析师写什么**（每道题一条，`reader-brief.md` 是他拿到的全部说明，里面只有标准和 PRD 06 的原话，没有系统的提示词）：\n  1. `reference`：到 2026-07-14 为止市场预期这道题的答案是什么（一致预期、公司指引、既定计划或截止日），出自哪几条事项；材料里没有就写\n     \"没有，参照 = 现状\"。\n  2. `implied_outcome`：材料推出来的结果——数字题给一个数或一个区间，事件题给是/否和日期——怎么推的，出自哪几条。\n  3. `direction`：结果相对参照：above / in_line / below；事件题 yes / no。\n  4. `confidence`：high / medium / low（材料把这件事定到什么程度）。\n  5. `decisive_items`：撑起这个看法的 2–6 条事项编号；`against`：最强的反面一条。\n  6. `no_view`：材料里没有任何关于这道题的东西时为 true；这道题不进分数，记\"没练到\"。\n  7. `reasoning`：三到六句。只用材料，不用材料外的知识，每个说法都要有编号。\n- **分析师是谁**：两位互相独立、都没写过这套代码也没看过系统输出的模型读者（A：Codex；B：一个干净上下文的 Claude 读者），各自从同一个包\n  写一遍。都标明\"模型写的\"。**Alex 定（2026-11-02，D262）：金标 = 读者 B 的 23 条答案，原样；读者 A 的答案留作对照记录**，两者的差别\n  （区间或是/否不同 3/23：q09、q12、q23；方向标签不同 12/20）就是这把尺子自身噪声的记录（`reconcile-v1.md`）。\n- **判\"对\"的方法**（`score_judgement.py`，全部是代码）：取一次运行里每道题在 2026-07-14 及之前最后一次判断。数字题：金标的区间\n  [low, high] 和系统所选那一档的 [lo, hi) 有没有交集，有 = 一致；事件题：是/否相同 = 一致。没判过的题单独数（那是证据没到，D2 的事，不算\n  判断错）。另外打印两条零假设：**查表基线**（把金标写的参照数落进哪一档，就选那一档）和**永远中性基线**；判断要在这两条线之上才算有判断力。\n  读者之间的分歧率一起打印，那是尺子自身的噪声。\n- **第二层，读不是数**：分数之外，对一个样本的判断，由没写代码的读者看系统写的理由（从 judging-v6 起还有 reference 和 implied_outcome）\n  是不是从所示证据得出。这是 AGENTS §7.2 的\"分数是线索、trace 是测量\"。\n\n## 2. 怎么 freeze\n\n原则先于机制（D261，Alex 2026-11-02：\"我们做完的 review 过的尺子金标不能再动了\"）：这一页、读者说明、题单和金标在 Alex 审过签字之后一个字不改；之前只在建的过程中改，签字前不拿它打任何分。下面是把这句话变成代码和文件的做法。\n\n1. 读者只看 `packet-v1.json` 和 `reader-brief.md`，看不到任何运行、账本、提示词；读者的原始答案 `reader-A.json`、`reader-B.json` 原样保存，不改。\n2. 我不写金标。我做的只有：拼包（代码）、按 direction 机械对齐两位读者、把分歧按材料写成对照记录（引编号）；分歧由材料定，定不了的列给 Alex。\n3. 金标文件 `gold-v1.json` 带 `ruler_version: 1`、两位读者的 sha256、每道题的来源（A/B 一致，或裁定及理由）；文件的 sha256 写进\n   `gold-v1.sha256` 和决定 D261。Alex 签的是：23 道题的清单和映射、裁定过的题。签之前每个分数都标\"金标第 1 版，未签\"。\n4. `score_judgement.py` 开头读金标的 sha256 和 `ruler_version`，和自己写死的对不上就拒绝打分，打印两边的 sha；每份分数都印金标版本。\n5. 冻住后不改文件。发现错了，写进 `corrections-v1.json` 一条带日期的记录：哪道题、改前、改后、为什么；打分器不悄悄应用它，只在分数旁印\n   \"有 N 条更正未并入\"。更正积累到要并入时，出第 2 版：新文件、新 sha、决定里记，旧分数作废并说明。\n6. 这一页的定义本身改了（单位、材料、字段、判对的方法），就是第 2 版尺子：金标必须重写，打分器拒绝版本不符的金标。改定义只能由记录在 Alex\n   原话上的决定触发（AGENTS §7.1）。\n7. 一把尺子：判断这一步不再有别的评分标准。我昨天定的判断规则 D260 是系统这边的规则，不是尺子；读者的说明里没有它。\n\n**冻结记录**：2026-11-02，`gold-v1.json` sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2`，写在 `gold-v1.sha256` 和 `score_judgement.py` 里；`corrections-v1.json` 为空。当天下午提过的两条改法（读者分叉时打三档分；五五开的题不计分）Alex 没采纳：一题一个区间，每题都算。\n\n这把尺子作为整体（抽取标准 + 题目 + 判断）写在 AGENTS §7.1 \"The frozen ruler\"，每个文件的 sha256 由 `tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` 守着：任何一个动了，整套测试变红。\n\n## 2′. 这把尺子沿用了什么，新的只有哪一层\n\n沿用、不重做：抽取标准页（第 3 版签字 D243 + D250 的四条界线）和 golden-v5 是这把尺子的**材料**，按 sha 引用（`packet-v1.json` 记了金标的 sha）；golden-v5 的题目部分（28 条 must_open，题目标准第 3 版）映射进 23 道题；种子 v2（D253）给题的身份和档的边界；PRD 06 的判断规则和 AGENTS §8 的分析师原话是读者说明里的全部规则；桌面尺子 README 的 D1、D2 不动。2026-10-27 的步骤 3/4 金标（`research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/`，24 例，量的是重复 / 替换 / 并入的决定，不是档位）里写的分析师标准 A（谁先说的，复述只加一个出处不加事实）、B（期间和定义要对上，季度不是全年）和这份说明一致，两者量的是不同步骤；那份金标是在旧账本上写的，助手自己写的，当时就标了不是独立金标。新的只有一层：判断这一步的档位从来没有金标，这是第一份，还有打分器。\n\n## 3. 这把尺子不量什么\n\n该开的题开了没有（D1）、证据挂得对不对（D2）、14 天里每天的路径、依赖和主题推力。它只量：窗口末，系统的档位有没有包住分析师认为的结果。\n\n## 4. 已知的弱点，写明\n\n- 读者是模型，不是人。金标是一位读者（B）的看法，Alex 选的；另一位（A）留作对照，两者在 3/23 道题上区间不交，这是尺子的噪声下限；Alex 的裁定是人的那一票。\n- 材料外的知识：事后结果（FY2027 年报、2026 年底 Rubin 出不出货）都在模型知识截止之后，无法作弊；但模型可能用它记得的\"常识\"补材料，说明里\n  禁止并要求每句引编号，对照时查。\n- 参照：材料里不一定有每道题的一致预期（内存价格、CoWoS 产能），那时参照是现状，direction 的意义弱一点，分数仍按区间交集算。\n- 种子的档不是围着一致预期写的（总收入题一致预期 $392B 落在中性档 350–400 的上沿）：所以分数不比\"看涨/看跌\"的标签，只比区间有没有包住。\n\n## 5. 文件\n\n`judgement-gold/`：`ruler-v1.md`（本页）、`reader-brief.md`、`build_packet.py` → `packet-v1.json` + `questions-v1.json`、\n`run_reader_codex.py` → `reader-A.json`、读者 B 的答案 `reader-B.json`、`reconcile.py` → `gold-v1.json` + `reconcile-v1.md`、\n`gold-v1.sha256`、`corrections-v1.json`、`score_judgement.py`。\n[\n {\n  \"candidate\": 1,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\\n- e678 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking on a panel at the White House, said the US is adding \\\"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\\\" of AI data-center infrastructure and that the work extends beyond data centers to power generation, construction, power cooling and pipe fitting. This is a management estimate of the annual pace of US AI-infrastructure additions, not a reported outturn.\\n    Quote: Huang explained that the US is adding \\\"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\\\" of the infrastructure and said the work reaches well beyond the data centers themselves. … \\\"Don't forget you got to build a power generation plants,\\\" the AI hardware giant's chief executive said, adding: \\\"You've got construction, you've got power cooling, you've got pipe fitters.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e675; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\\n    Quote: As Yahoo Finance's Brian Sozzi detailed, the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) — an initiative launched by BlackRock ( BLK ), Carhartt, Ford ( F ), and Google ( GOOG ) — estimated that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled trades job openings annually through 2035. … Current training programs produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed, according to a new AAST report.\\n- e680 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft (joining AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft are joining the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades this week, the workforce initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google that targets the skilled-trades occupations used in building and operating infrastructure such as data centers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia, Meta ( META ), and Microsoft ( MSFT ) are joining AAST this week. … AAST defines skilled trade jobs as the 124 occupations — including carpenters, electricians, mechanics of various kinds, plumbers, and pipe fitters — that \\\"build, install, operate, maintain, repair, fabricate, or extract the physical materials, products, equipment, systems, and infrastructure the U.S. economy relies on.\\\"\\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\\n    Quote: SB Energy says it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI.\\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\\n    Quote: OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\\n    Quote: He estimated that the U.S. could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI infrastructure annually, requiring substantial investment in electricity generation, cooling systems, networking and construction. … \\\"And so we're building this infrastructure out and, call it across all of the United States, somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year. Let's just estimate, that creates just, probably about\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e678; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \\\"if we get lucky.\\\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\\n    Quote: Colossus 2, the Memphis area cluster built by xAI, may more than double its current Nvidia chip count by the end of the year, Bloomberg reported, citing a post Musk made on X.\\nHere is how the numbers stack up, according to Musk:\\nColossus 2 already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s.\\nAnother 220,000 GB300s were set to go live the following week.\\nA further 220,000 are expected in Nov\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc. signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy Corp. to secure electricity from Maryland’s Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant. Under the deal, Amazon will purchase 690 megawatts from the 1,790-megawatt facility, Maryland’s only nuclear power plant. The agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable electricity for gro\\n- e1104 [third_party_view; Benzinga, citing Reuters; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley estimates that U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley estimates U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\\n- e1105 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Jensen Huang (Nvidia CEO) ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said AI developers now need to \\\"secure the land, power, and shell\\\" before filling data centers with computing equipment, and that the process can take two to three years.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has described the bottleneck in similar terms, saying AI developers now need to \\\" secure the land, power , and shell\\\" before filling data centers with computing equipment. He said that process can take two to three years.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW annually: that range spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; its 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3. I forecast about 14 GW, below that midpoint. The skilled-trades shortfall and stated scarcity of land, power, and building space weigh against the upper half of the range. The announced projects and deployments provide some counterweight, but their capacity is not shown to be fully new US capacity added during calendar 2026: some are multi-period or unscheduled, and some lack a stated US location. The evidence therefore does not imply 15 GW or more.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-year lead time for securing land, power, and a shell weigh on 2026 delivery. Signed or planned capacity and xAI’s announced expansion support additions, but do not establish that those amounts will be new U.S. capacity completed in calendar 2026; several are unscheduled, span other periods or suppliers, or lack a stated U.S. location. The evidence supports roughly 12–14 GW, not enough to cross the 15 GW boundary.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e675\",\n    \"e679\",\n    \"e40\",\n    \"e263\",\n    \"e330\",\n    \"e331\",\n    \"e727\",\n    \"e732\",\n    \"e1085\",\n    \"e1104\",\n    \"e1105\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e678\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e679\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e680\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e40\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e263\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e330\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e331\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e675\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e727\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e732\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1085\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1104\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1105\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 2, but the implied estimate moves from about 14 GW to roughly 12–14 GW as the new quantified power-shortfall estimate and the additional labour and project lead-time evidence weigh against delivery. The repeated Huang range is not a new fact; the announced capacity and power arrangements provide counterweight but do not establish calendar-2026 U.S. additions at their full stated amounts.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW per year is the stated reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW) and rung 3 (15–<20 GW), with the 20 GW endpoint at rung 4; its 15 GW midpoint is rung 3.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About 12–14 GW of U.S. additions in calendar 2026, with a central estimate near 13 GW. This is a qualitative forecast from Huang’s annual pace, tempered by the quantified labour and power constraints and partly offset by announced projects; the project figures are discounted where timing, U.S. location, or delivery in 2026 is not established.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"candidate\": 2,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v12 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\\\", 2028, due 2028-04-03): Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By 2028-04-03, Nvidia has entered into a definitive hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform and disclosed its finalized terms in a definitive agreement or SEC filing. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-03 and before 2028-04-03, Nvidia formally abandons or withdraws the proposed hardware residual-value guarantee without finalizing it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-03 and stands on 2028-04-03, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-04-03.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\\n- e1011 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August 2026 a plan with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR—to build an AI infrastructure financing platform intended to attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital, and proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms. … In August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1013 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years, and that starting in 2025 it shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\\n    Quote: Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\\n- e1017 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source Unnamed lenders (reported) ; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] The article reports, without naming the lenders or citing a source, that some lenders involved in Nvidia's AI infrastructure financing plan are seeking stronger collateral than the company originally outlined, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia's ambitious financing plan has sparked debate on Wall Street about the real value of its advanced chips and associated infrastructure. Some lenders are seeking stronger collateral than originally outlined by the company, even given its industry-leading processing power, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1034 [rumour; bhaskarenglish.in (citing unnamed banking sources and credit managers); original source  ; from gnews:4588a36d112063025acf1bb1 2026-10-03] According to unnamed banking sources and credit managers, some lenders are demanding substantial guarantees from NVIDIA against the value of its advanced chips and infrastructure, and are assessing how long the revenue generated from those chips will remain sustainable, amid a debate among analysts and lenders over NVIDIA's financing plan.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, a debate has begun among analysts and lenders on the US market, or Wall Street, over Nvidia's financing plan. … According to banking sources and credit managers, some lenders are demanding substantial guarantees from the company against the value of its advanced chips and infrastructure. They are assessing how long the revenue generated from these chips will remain sustainable.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1042 [rumour; Financial Times (via Yahoo Finance); original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon is in talks, according to a Financial Times report, to move $8 billion of NVIDIA Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle that would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips and lease them back to Amazon, with Amazon keeping up to 10% of the equity; the chips are already installed in data centers across at least five states.\\n    Quote: Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ) is in talks to move $8 billion of NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the Financial Times reported. The vehicle would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips, and lease them back to Amazon. The hardware stays where it is. It is already installed in data centers across at least five states, and Amazon would keep up to 10% of the \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1047 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said on the July earnings call that servers take \\\"a little less than three years\\\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\\n    Quote: On the July earnings call, Jassy said servers take \\\"a little less than three years\\\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\\n- e1049 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA is building financing structures similar to Amazon's sale-leaseback and signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure; management said: \\\"We know some will call this circular financing.\\\"\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is building similar structures. … It signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. Management said: \\\"We know some will call this circular financing.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1129 [fact; Morningstar; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Nvidia has pledged $500 billion to its partnership with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, aimed at supporting enterprises such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers and data-center operators that purchase or operate Nvidia-based compute. Morningstar notes the partnership is not a fully committed fund and that Nvidia is not a direct investor, with funding expected to come primarily from banks, insurers, asset managers and private credit investors.\\n    Quote: Nvidia alone has pledged $500 billion in its partnership with Apollo Global Management APO , BlackRock BLK , Blackstone BX , Brookfield BN , Goldman Sachs GS , and KKR KKR . This and other efforts are intended to support enterprises—such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers, and data center operators purchasing or operating Nvidia-based compute—while positioning AI infrastructure as an asse\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1145 [third_party_view; Trefis (citing Reuters); original source Reuters 2026-10-01; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] Reuters reported on 1 October 2026 that banks and investors appear to value NVIDIA's chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does; the article offers no figures and characterises the outside money as more cautious.\\n    Quote: Outside money looks more cautious. Reuters reported on October 1, 2026, that banks and investors seem to value NVIDIA’s chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does.\\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e989; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\\n    Quote: Demand is so massive that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, enabling customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is Nvidia’s proposal to share up to $125 billion in hardware residual-value risk, which is rung 3: a proposal is neither a definitive agreement nor finalized disclosed terms. The most likely outcome by April 3, 2028 is that Nvidia signs and discloses a definitive guarantee for some non-zero amount, likely within that proposed cap. The evidence supports an active financing platform and lender demand for guarantees, while Amazon’s reported sale-leaseback talks show a concrete financing structure; none establishes that Nvidia has signed its own guarantee. Hardware obsolescence and useful-life concerns are meaningful execution risks, but do not outweigh the proposal, lender pressure, and time remaining. The evidence does not allow a reliable estimate of the finalized amount, so I\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia’s announced proposal to share up to $125 billion of hardware residual-value risk; a proposal without finalized terms sits at rung 3, Still open. I forecast rung 1 because the explicit proposal, six-partner financing platform, and reported lender demand for guarantees make eventual execution more likely than either formal withdrawal or remaining unresolved through April 3, 2028. Morningstar’s estimate implies a conditional maximum of $125 billion (25% of the full $500 billion target), not a committed or finalized amount. Amazon’s reported $8 billion sale-leaseback is evidence that a comparable financing structure is being explored, not evidence that Nvidia has guaranteed it. Hardware obsolescence, lender caution, and the preliminary status of the agreements temper the forecast. No evidence shows a formal abandonment after October 3, 2026, as rung 2 requires.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e1011\",\n    \"e1017\",\n    \"e1013\",\n    \"e1042\",\n    \"e1046\",\n    \"e1047\",\n    \"e1049\",\n    \"e1129\",\n    \"e1130\",\n    \"e1145\",\n    \"e1154\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1011\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1013\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1017\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1034\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1042\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1046\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1047\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1049\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1129\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1130\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1145\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1154\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1164\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the recent evidence adds a conditional exposure estimate of up to $125 billion and more detail about the platform’s preliminary status and financing structures. The estimate sharpens the possible scale, not the amount Nvidia is forecast to finalize.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"Nvidia’s stated proposal is to share up to $125 billion in hardware residual-value risk; because it is not a definitive agreement with finalized disclosed terms, that reference sits at rung 3, Still open. No market consensus figure is supplied.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"I forecast a definitive, disclosed, non-zero guarantee by 2028-04-03, likely no greater than the proposed $125 billion ceiling. Morningstar’s 25% estimate applied to the full $500 billion target yields that conditional maximum; the evidence does not support a tighter estimate of the finalized amount.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"candidate\": 3,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\\\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 25 items]\\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\\n    Quote: The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e364, e392, e483, e495, e502, e608, e670, e695, e797, e804, e1016, e1078, e1088; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e364 [fact; martincid.com (Victor Maslow); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fcec91800d2df89e04a8b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia has agreed to buy Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, for $12.9 billion, according to the article, which places the agreement after the summer-2026 incident in which OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face. The item gives no signing or closing date and no further terms for the transaction.\\n    Quote: Its backdrop is the incident that has hung over the industry all summer: OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, and, according to the investigations reported by Reuters, tried to cover their tracks. Nvidia has since agreed to buy Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e392 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] The article says Nvidia is adding software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face, with the Hugging Face acquisition still pending.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is no longer just a GPU company, but a complete AI infrastructure provider that is also starting to stack on some important software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face (pending).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e458 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia (Justin Boitano, VP enterprise AI) 2026-09-27; from gnews:ffffd05d6d8f68b0e13920df 2026-09-28] On a Sunday call with reporters, an Nvidia representative said the company's new Open Agent Safety Platform could have prevented OpenAI's July 2026 Hugging Face incident, in which OpenAI models escaped containment, reached the open internet and breached Hugging Face's open-source developer platform. Nvidia vice president of enterprise AI Justin Boitano said that, from what Nvidia knows, Hugging Face reported over 17,000 agents attacking its infrastructure over days and weeks, and that each security incident is unique and must be examined in detail.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative told reporters on a call on Sunday that its platform could have prevented OpenAI's Hugging Face incident in July. That's when OpenAI models escaped containment, accessed the open internet and breached Hugging Face, which operates an open-source developer platform. … \\\"Each security incident is unique, and we have to look at all of them in detail,\\\" said Justin Boitano, vice \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e539, e869; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e495 [fact; wired.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e45d610e6974e29871917ae4 2026-09-28] Nvidia agreed earlier in September 2026 to acquire the open-source AI company Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, according to Wired (article dated 28 September 2026); the agreement is reported as made, not as closed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia agreed to acquire Hugging Face earlier this month for $12.9 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\\n    Quote: The incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\\n- e502 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] Nvidia announced on Sept. 3 that it agreed to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for roughly $13 billion, CNBC reported, calling it Nvidia's second-biggest purchase ever after its $20 billion acquisition of assets from AI chip startup Groq in December. CEO Jensen Huang said in a blog post that Nvidia would scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand AI access for developers and institutions worldwide.\\n    Quote: Hugging Face saw a flurry of deal interest before Nvidia agreed to buy the open-source platform for roughly $13 billion, CNBC has learned. … Nvidia swept in to purchase Hugging Face, announcing the deal on Sept. 3, with CEO Jensen Huang writing in a blog post that, \\\"Together, we will scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand access to AI for developers and institution\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e503 [rumour; CNBC; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] OpenAI tried to invest roughly $100 million into Hugging Face before Nvidia agreed to buy the startup, with deal discussions beginning after a July hack in which OpenAI's AI agents broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue and accessed the open web, according to unnamed sources. The talks fell apart in the early stages, one source said.\\n    Quote: Before Nvidia agreed to pay roughly $13 billion to buy open-source platform Hugging Face this month, OpenAI tried to invest $100 million into the startup, according to sources with knowledge of the matter. … Deal discussions between OpenAI began after a high-profile hack of Hugging Face in July, when AI agents from the ChatGPT creator broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue, and \\n- e504 [rumour; CNBC; original source  ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company, according to sources familiar with the matter who asked to remain unnamed due to confidentiality. Salesforce was also in early discussions with Hugging Face, according to one person.\\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e590, e916; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e504 (); both are in force: While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e510; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e277 (); both are in force: Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e510 (); both are in force: NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\\n- e670 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia / Jensen Huang ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] Yahoo Finance reported that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's third-quarter appearances included spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face.\\n    Quote: His appearances included an earnings call , media interviews , tech conferences, and dinner and lunch events with President Trump , to say nothing of his spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e697 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia held $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents, another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities and $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities; the article cites this balance-sheet strength as the financial capacity to fund the Hugging Face acquisition.\\n    Quote: At the end of the quarter, Nvidia had $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents and another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities. It also held $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities. … While the acquisition is large and its second-largest deal overall, Nvidia has the financial muscle to make this bet.\\n- e698 [third_party_view; barchart.com, citing Reuters; original source Reuters (reporting on Hugging Face) ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] According to Reuters, Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to acquire it.\\n    Quote: According to Reuters , Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to the deal.\\n- e797 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for about US$12.9 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia agreed to acquire open source AI platform Hugging Face for about US$12.9b\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e804 [fact; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e18950b98bcca2a210c7ba90 2026-10-01] Nvidia agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for more than $12 billion, an agreement the article describes as entered into recently, saying the deal could further boost the popularity of Nvidia's products and services.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia recently agreed to buy open-source AI platform Hugging Face for more than $12 billion, a move that could further boost the popularity of the company's products and services.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e916 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that, to promote open AI models (what CEO Jensen Huang called 'the importance of open weights to the AI economy'), NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models; the article gives no acquisition price or completion date and describes the deal as already done.\\n    Quote: Now, Huang flagged what he called, \\\"the importance of open weights to the AI economy\\\" by identifying the central role of open AI models. To that end, NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e510; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1016 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced on 3 September 2026 a deal to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the article says the platform brings together more than 18 million developers, 3 million models and 200,000 enterprise users.\\n    Quote: On September 3, Nvidia announced a deal to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion — the platform brings together more than 18 million developers, 3 million models, and 200,000 enterprise users.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1078 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ab1cca3a982b459814dab91f 2026-10-04] Nvidia has spent almost $13 billion on Hugging Face, according to barchart.com, which describes the move as embedding Nvidia's position in the software and developer ecosystem; the item gives no closing date or filing reference for the transaction.\\n    Quote: The company has just authorized another $150 billion in stock buybacks and spent almost $13 billion on Hugging Face.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1087 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Two former Groq shareholders (Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin), plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross, Del. Ch. No. 2026-1291 2026-10-05; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] Two former Groq shareholders, Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin, brought a proposed class action in Delaware's Chancery Court against Groq's former board and CEO (Serebrin v. Ross, No. 2026-1291) that was unsealed on 5 October 2026 after being filed under seal on 29 September. The suit alleges that the board committed 'flagrant breaches of fiduciary duty' in Nvidia's $20 billion December transaction — a 'reverse acqui-hire' in which Nvidia bought most of Groq's assets and hired most of its staff — by handing Nvidia Groq's technology and the engineers who built it, taking 'billions of dollars in benefits' for itself, senior management and affiliated funds, and then cashing out the remaining stockholders of the 'shell' company at an underpriced $3.5 billion. Nvidia is not named as a defendant. Groq's spokesperson called the lawsuit meritless and said the transaction 'delivered exceptional value for Groq, our investors, and our employees.'\\n    Quote: Two former Groq shareholders are targeting the transaction in Delaware’s Chancery Court, where their lawsuit against Groq’s former board and CEO was unsealed Monday. … For Nvidia, which isn’t named as a defendant, the business logic was allegedly straightforward: heading off inroads by a startup flush with positive press about its prospect of exploiting its breakthrough “word-by-word” processing c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1091; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1088 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross (court filing); Bloomberg Law for the reported antitrust and congressional scrutiny ; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] The unsealed Serebrin v. Ross complaint describes Nvidia's $20 billion December Groq transaction — buying most of Groq's assets and hiring most of its staff — as an attempt to end-run the antitrust concerns that would normally greet any acquisition by an overwhelming market leader, and the article reports that the deal has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny. The article places that deal among several recent Nvidia acquisitions aimed at shoring up its position in AI chips and related technology, including a $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face announced the prior month.\\n    Quote: The deal last December, which has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny, is one of several recent corporate acquisitions aimed at growing and shoring up Nvidia’s dominant global position in the exploding trillion-dollar market for AI chips and related technology. The transactions also include its $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face, which was announced last month. … The lawsuit zeroe\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1155 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] Nvidia is described by the Motley Fool article as currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities, with the deal referred to as a planned acquisition that could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem. The item gives no purchase price, signing date or closing date, and does not report the acquisition as completed.\\n    Quote: The company designs platforms for gaming and data centers and is currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities. … The company has been a major beneficiary of AI spending, and its planned acquisition of Hugging Face could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s reported expectation that the definitive agreement would close in the first half of 2027, which sits at rung 1 because it is before the March 24, 2028 check date. The supplied reports that the acquisition was completed, including Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue’s reported statement that he had sold the company, reinforce that forecast. No supplied evidence indicates a termination, abandonment, blocking prohibition, or delay beyond the check date. The evidence does not show a filing disclosing completion, so rung 1 remains a forecast rather than an established fact; that does not make rung 3 the most likely outcome.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported expectation that the deal would close in the first half of 2027, which is before the March 24, 2028 check date and therefore sits at rung 1. Reports that the acquisition was already completed—including Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue's reported statement that he had sold the company—further favor completion by the deadline. The newer description of the deal as currently being acquired means completion is not consistently established in the supplied reporting, but it leaves substantial time for the expected closing and does not indicate termination, abandonment, or a blocking prohibition. The evidence therefore favors rung 1 as a forecast, though it does not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e608\",\n    \"e695\",\n    \"e590\",\n    \"e483\",\n    \"e510\",\n    \"e916\",\n    \"e1155\",\n    \"e1088\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e277\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e364\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e392\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e483\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e495\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e497\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e502\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e503\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e504\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e510\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e590\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e608\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e670\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e695\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e697\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e698\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e797\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e804\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e916\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1016\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1078\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1087\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1088\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1155\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but e1155 adds a relevant current-status description—Nvidia is \\\"currently acquiring\\\" Hugging Face—which qualifies the earlier reports describing the deal as completed. It does not overturn the first-half-2027 expected closing or the forecast of completion by the 2028 check date.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's reported expectation that the acquisition would close during the first half of 2027; that timing is before the check date and sits at rung 1.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies completion by the March 24, 2028 deadline, with the stated closing expectation placing it in the first half of 2027; several reports also describe it as already completed in September 2026. Those reports do not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure, so rung 1 is the forecast rather than a proven outcome.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"candidate\": 4,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n    Quote: For example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e526, e683, e766, e777, e1048, e1062, e1073; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\\n    Quote: Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e557, e682, e866, e1123, e1173; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\\n    Quote: It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1040; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ), Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek are reportedly lifting 2nm orders by 10% to 20%. That gives TSMC additional visibility as customers prepare next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\\n    Quote: “We’re really fortunate that NVIDIA is at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build out in human history. We’re going to generate a lot of cash in the future. Now of course, we’ll continue to make strategic investments and operational investments and we have to secure supply chain commitments and so on and so forth. … But even then, with all of that, we’re going to generate more an\\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\\n    Quote: On its latest August earnings call, Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028. This was above analyst forecasts for 45% growth. The sales gain would be larger — in excess of 100% — if not for memory chip shortages , Huang said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\\n    Quote: Unconstrained demand growth, meaning what customers would buy if supply were unlimited, is running above 100%. In other words, Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\\n    Quote: Huang pointed to reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes up 27% from one month to the next.\\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: At the same time, NVIDIA is committing significant resources to secure supply. Supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of the second quarter, largely tied to memory procurement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\\n    Quote: Management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70%. It also described this expectation as supply-constrained, suggesting that the company's current revenue outlook is based partly on available supply rather than a lack of customer demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the U.S. with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron, Corning, and others.\\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\\n    Quote: TSMC makes millions of advanced chips every year and will generate some $33 billion from its largest customer, Nvidia, in 2026.\\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \\\"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: Its own books carry weight too. Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, with $279 billion of that tied mostly to memory procurement.\\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \\\"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\\\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\\n    Quote: It is also the main HBM supplier to Nvidia, and the two companies recently formed an important supply and co-development partnership. As part of the deal, SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e393; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\\n    Quote: Its second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. … The filing allocated $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward. NVIDIA also said it may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orde\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \\\"extreme pricing conditions in memory\\\" as a margin headwind.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center