AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The implied amount is well above rung 5's $125bn floor, so it remains strongly bearish on this ladder. The reported $279bn is a current supply/capacity commitment balance, not the FY2027 year-end amount; the larger multi-year HBM estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (neutral); no consensus or company forecast for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The evidence instead points well above rung 5's $125bn floor: NVIDIA reported $279bn of supply and capacity commitments at FY2027 Q2, including $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. A simple run-off benchmark is therefore about $187bn ($279bn less $92bn), before any new commitments or cancellations. Ongoing supply constraints and strong guided growth make replenishment plausible, but do not quantify it; the separate $500bn multi-year memory estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified. Even the disclosed run-off benchmark remains in rung 5.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e202\",\n    \"e1062\",\n    \"e953\",\n    \"e1040\",\n    \"e106\",\n    \"e236\",\n    \"e1027\",\n    \"e730\",\n    \"e732\",\n    \"e44\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e44\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e106\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e108\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e113\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e136\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e202\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e236\",