ne\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e944\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e946\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e968\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e970\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e987\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e997\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e998\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1003\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1012\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1019\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1020\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1022\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1024\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1025\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1026\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1027\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1028\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1030\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1031\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1074\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1083\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1107\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1108\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1123\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1132\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1150\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1151\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1163\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1169\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1173\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1188\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1189\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1190\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1191\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting facts are now more specific: the evidence adds Micron's stated 2027–2028 demand-supply outlook, its quantified customer commitments and committed 2027 output, and further reported pricing pressure. These update the supporting facts and figures without supplying a direct full-year contract-price figure.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price figure is supplied. The opening no-reference position is rung 3: 10% to below 20%.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"candidate\": 7,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\\\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The supplied market-facing reference is GF Securities’ forecast of 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in 2027, exactly the rung 1 boundary. Because this is a forecast for a product with no shipment volume shown in hand, it is discounted: the supplier evidence supports optical demand but does not establish Nvidia CPO switch unit volume or convert to 100,000 Nvidia switches. The most likely outcome is therefore below 100,000, while no evidence shows a formal abandonment or other decision against the event as a whole; rung 3 is the best forecast.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e1068\",\n    \"e1069\",\n    \"e1070\",\n    \"e1071\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1068\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1069\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1070\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1071\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"No change in rung or key supporting facts: the 100,000-unit figure remains a third-party forecast, and supplier growth and optical-revenue estimates still do not establish Nvidia's shipment volume or a decision to abandon CPO.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"GF Securities' forecast of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027 is the market-facing reference; 100,000 is the rung 1 boundary.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"candidate\": 8,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 53 items]\\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e85 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:44b5d56e3c6718335d33ab2b 2026-09-23] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning the company to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment, as reported by Zacks.\\n    Quote: Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90, e185, e251, e283, e291, e320, e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e89 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nebius Group; CoreWeave ; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong.\\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\\n    Quote: It's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e859, e951; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: During SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, Musk had this to say about SpaceX's relationship with Nvidia: … We've decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with NVIDIA. So, we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e387, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234, e409, e598, e650, e721, e857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. … The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\\n    Quote: The trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\\n    Quote: In the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x. … Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\\n    Quote: The company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e555, e764, e782, e813, e858; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c