e Akamai Technologies 2026-09-24; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai Technologies said on 24 September 2026 that it had significantly expanded its partnership with Anthropic; Anthropic committed to spend $11.6 billion over seven years on dedicated cloud capacity and related services for its growing CPU workloads, with an option to commit an additional $9 billion if conditions are met, bringing the total value to about $20 billion. The article says the deal does not directly involve Nvidia based on public disclosure.\\n    Quote: A major new agreement between cloud infrastructure provider Akamai Technologies (AKAM) and frontier AI lab Anthropic could add another piece to that story. The nearly $12 billion deal does not directly involve Nvidia, at least based on what has been publicly disclosed so far. … On Sept. 24, cloud infrastructure company Akamai Technologies significantly expanded its partnership with frontier AI lab\\n- e979 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source GF Securities (Henry Huang) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] GF Securities analyst Henry Huang said the Akamai-Anthropic CPU cloud deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which he said Akamai had previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads; the article presents this as an expectation, not confirmation.\\n    Quote: \\\"The deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which Akamai previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads,\\\" according to GF Securities analyst Henry Huang.\\n- e980 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Nvidia said in an August 2026 blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic; the article also notes Nvidia had named Anthropic among AI labs planning to adopt Vera at GTC Taipei in May. The delivery is reported as completed, but no volume or revenue is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said at GTC Taipei that Anthropic was among the AI labs planning to adopt Vera, and the chipmaker added in an August blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic.\\n- e981 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion of capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components; the article says not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs because Akamai also needs memory, ABF substrates and other components.\\n    Quote: Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion in capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components. It is worth noting that not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs, as Akamai will also need to purchase memory, ABF substrates, and other components.\\n- e982 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion and that next-generation Vera was in full production; she said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM, continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs, and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double year-over-year in fiscal 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said on the Q2 earnings call last month that trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion, while next-generation Vera was in full production. Kress said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, adding that the company continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs. She also\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e632; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1060 [fact; Brett Schafer, Fool.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:654a1658fd6ce4b1694951fc 2026-10-04] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the sole at-scale manufacturer of Nvidia's chips, reported revenue of $143 billion over the past 12 months and revenue up 34% year over year in the most recent quarter, with its high-performance-compute (HPC) segment rising 20% quarter over quarter and now at 66% of the overall business, per Fool.com's account.\\n    Quote: Revenue was $143 billion during the past 12 months and rose 34% year-over-year last quarter. The company is seeing booming demand from its high performance compute (HPC) segment, which increased 20% quarter-over-quarter and now makes up 66% of the overall business.\\n- e1098 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) 2026-10-05; from gnews:3b878790fc298c0b29ba8205 2026-10-05] Foxconn, described as Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter 2026 revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion — a supplier volume signal for Nvidia's AI server business, though the item does not specify which Nvidia platform drove it.\\n    Quote: This morning, Foxconn, Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1101 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA (product status reported by fool.com) ; from gnews:a6cfdb974af32e5f975e0d4f 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's GPU release cadence continues: after Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years, the release of the current Vera Rubin system is now unfolding, according to fool.com's 5 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has pledged to update its GPUs on an annual basis, and it's following through on this, with Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years and the current release of the Vera Rubin system now unfolding.\\n- e1125 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said each new product generation brings in more money for the same data-center capacity, putting revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Each new product generation also brings in more money for the same amount of data center capacity. Management put revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1148 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA itself expects volume sales to double, underpinned by the Vera Rubin release.\\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\\n- e1178 [party_statement; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence said it plans to bring an additional 300 MW of compute capacity to market in the next 15 months, requiring capital expenditure of more than $20 billion on top of the $6 billion already committed for its initial 100 MW. It said it has developed a pipeline of about 400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia, that the three Rubin GPU orders are the first committed tranche of that pipeline, and that the balance is under development for delivery in subsequent phases in 2027 and early 2028.\\n    Quote: AMI plans to bring additional 300 MWs of capacity to market in the next 15 months, involving capital expenditure of more than $20+ billion in addition to the $6 billion already committed for the initial $100 MW. … AMI has developed a pipeline of ~400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across global locations, including India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia. The three orders placed to date\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, which is rung 1 because Q3 falls within FY2027. That guidance alone is a forecast, not proof of recognized revenue. But the evidence also says production shipments began in August, Supermicro is shipping Vera Rubin racks, and CoreWeave has the platform running production workloads for Cognition; these make the guided Q3 revenue share more likely to be realized and disclosed by the due date. The supply-bottleneck and possible-delay evidence is a counterweight, but does not outweigh the near-term guidance and customer deployment evidence. The updated capacity and DRAM themes add further caution; they do not move the forecast below rung 1.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e410\",\n    \"e555\",\n    \"e764\",\n    \"e858\",\n    \"e857\",\n    \"e181\",\n    \"e659\",\n    \"e660\",\n    \"e895\",\n    \"e416\",\n    \"e1177\",\n    \"e236\",\n    \"e279\",\n    \"e864\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e85\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e89\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e90\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e181\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e188\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e208\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e236\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e243\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e279\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e283\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e370\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e409\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e410\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e416\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e551\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e555\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e633\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e659\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e660\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e720\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e753\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e764\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e765\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e775\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e782\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e811\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e813\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e814\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e832\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e857\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e858\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e859\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e860\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e864\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e894\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e895\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e907\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e951\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e978\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e979\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e980\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e981\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e982\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1046\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1060\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1098\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1101\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1125\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1148\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1177\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1178\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the newly disclosed firm orders for about 20,000 additional Rubin GPUs, bringing AMI's commitments to about 29,000, add a specific customer-demand fact to the supporting picture. Their stated Q2 2027 delivery schedule is beyond this thesis's deadline, so they strengthen the demand context without changing the implied Q3 FY2027 revenue outcome.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v11\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. This is the rung 1 reference: Q3 results are expected before the 2027-01-31 due date.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.\"\n  }\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\")); [(print(x[\\\"candidate\\\"],len(x[\\\"request\\\"]), x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")[0],json.dumps({k:v for k,v in x[\\\"answer\\\"].items() if k in [\\\"rung\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\")); [(print(x[\\\"candidate\\\"],len(x[\\\"request\\\"]), x[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")[0],json.dumps({k:v for k,v in x[\\\"answer\\\"].items() if k in [\\\"rung\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"1 11733 Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports? {\"rung\": 2, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-year lead time for securing land, power, and a shell weigh on 2026 delivery. Signed or planned capacity and xAI’s announced expansion support additions, but do not establish that those amounts will be new U.S. capacity completed in calendar 2026; several are unscheduled, span other periods or suppliers, or lack a stated U.S. location. The evidence supports roughly 12–14 GW, not enough to cross the 15 GW boundary.\", \"reference\": \"Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW per year is the stated reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW) and rung 3 (15–<20 GW), with the 20 GW endpoint at rung 4; its 15 GW midpoint is rung 3.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About 12–14 GW of U.S. additions in calendar 2026, with a central estimate near 13 GW. This is a qualitative forecast from Huang’s annual pace, tempered by the quantified labour and power constraints and partly offset by announced projects; the project figures are discounted where timing, U.S. location, or delivery in 2026 is not established.\"}\n2 17115 Thesis v12 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\", 2028, due 2028-04-03): Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03? {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia’s announced proposal to share up to $125 billion of hardware residual-value risk; a proposal without finalized terms sits at rung 3, Still open. I forecast rung 1 because the explicit proposal, six-partner financing platform, and reported lender demand for guarantees make eventual execution more likely than either formal withdrawal or remaining unresolved through April 3, 2028. Morningstar’s estimate implies a conditional maximum of $125 billion (25% of the full $500 billion target), not a committed or finalized amount. Amazon’s reported $8 billion sale-leaseback is evidence that a comparable financing structure is being explored, not evidence that Nvidia has guaranteed it. Hardware obsolescence, lender caution, and the preliminary status of the agreements temper the forecast. No evidence shows a formal abandonment after October 3, 2026, as rung 2 requires.\", \"reference\": \"Nvidia’s stated proposal is to share up to $125 billion in hardware residual-value risk; because it is not a definitive agreement with finalized disclosed terms, that reference sits at rung 3, Still open. No market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"I forecast a definitive, disclosed, non-zero guarantee by 2028-04-03, likely no greater than the proposed $125 billion ceiling. Morningstar’s 25% estimate applied to the full $500 billion target yields that conditional maximum; the evidence does not support a tighter estimate of the finalized amount.\"}\n3 26915 Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings? {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported expectation that the deal would close in the first half of 2027, which is before the March 24, 2028 check date and therefore sits at rung 1. Reports that the acquisition was already completed—including Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue's reported statement that he had sold the company—further favor completion by the deadline. The newer description of the deal as currently being acquired means completion is not consistently established in the supplied reporting, but it leaves substantial time for the expected closing and does not indicate termination, abandonment, or a blocking prohibition. The evidence therefore favors rung 1 as a forecast, though it does not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure.\", \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's reported expectation that the acquisition would close during the first half of 2027; that timing is before the check date and sits at rung 1.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies completion by the March 24, 2028 deadline, with the stated closing expectation placing it in the first half of 2027; several reports also describe it as already completed in September 2026. Those reports do not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure, so rung 1 is the forecast rather than a proven outcome.\"}\n4 26462 Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure? {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (neutral); no consensus or company forecast for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The evidence instead points well above rung 5's $125bn floor: NVIDIA reported $279bn of supply and capacity commitments at FY2027 Q2, including $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. A simple run-off benchmark is therefore about $187bn ($279bn less $92bn), before any new commitments or cancellations. Ongoing supply constraints and strong guided growth make replenishment plausible, but do not quantify it; the separate $500bn multi-year memory estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified. Even the disclosed run-off benchmark remains in rung 5.\", \"reference\": \"No market consensus or company guidance figure for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The reference is therefore the opening no-reference rung, rung 3 (neutral: $75bn to below $100bn).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $187bn as a disclosed-schedule run-off benchmark: $279bn of Q2 commitments less the $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. New commitments and cancellations could move the year-end balance, but the evidence does not quantify those changes; the likely outcome remains above $125bn.\"}\n5 78923 Thesis v3 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures? {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell exports and no Chinese allowance for those shipments by January 31, 2027: current-generation chips remain restricted, and Chinese authorities have restricted even U.S.-permitted H200 shipments. Reports of B300 servers in China concern deployments for which U.S. regulations require an explicit license, not evidence that both governments have authorized shipments. No supplied evidence establishes a post-September 23, 2026 formal prohibition that stands through the due date, so the evidence does not cross into rung 2.\", \"reference\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion, excluding China Data Center compute revenue. That reference sits at rung 3, Still open; it is a near-term revenue assumption, not a formal ruling on Blackwell authorization by January 31, 2027.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"By January 31, 2027, the most likely outcome is that Blackwell exports have not received verified U.S. authorization together with Chinese permission for Nvidia shipments. Existing restrictions and Chinese limits on H200 shipments support that forecast, but the evidence does not show a qualifying post-September 23 formal prohibition, leaving the outcome at rung 3.\"}\n6 51019 Thesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data? {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028, while more than 75% of its 2027 output is committed. Micron's strong results and explicit reports of extreme memory pricing reinforce the direction. Together these make a 30%+ annual average contract-price increase the most likely rung, though the evidence does not provide a direct contract-price series or a precise percentage. Consumer-product price rises and memory-cost comments are supporting context, not measurements of DRAM/HBM contract prices; the deployment-delay risk is a counterweight.\", \"reference\": \"No market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price figure is supplied. The opening no-reference position is rung 3: 10% to below 20%.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.\"}\n7 9560 Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027? {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\", \"reference\": \"GF Securities' forecast of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027 is the market-facing reference; 100,000 is the rung 1 boundary.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\"}\n8 45840 Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K? {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\", \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. This is the rung 1 reference: Q3 results are expected before the 2027-01-31 due date.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"candidate\\\"]); s=x[\\\"request\\\"]; s=re.sub(r\\\"    Quote:.*?(?=\\\\n- e|\\\\n\\\\[|\\\\Z)\\\",\\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); s=re.sub(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\[Themes in force.*\\\", \\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); print(s)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"candidate\\\"]); s=x[\\\"request\\\"]; s=re.sub(r\\\"    Quote:.*?(?=\\\\n- e|\\\\n\\\\[|\\\\Z)\\\",\\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); s=re.sub(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\[Themes in force.*\\\", \\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); print(s)'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### 1\nThesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\n- e678 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking on a panel at the White House, said the US is adding \"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\" of AI data-center infrastructure and that the work extends beyond data centers to power generation, construction, power cooling and pipe fitting. This is a management estimate of the annual pace of US AI-infrastructure additions, not a reported outturn.\n\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\n\n- e680 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft (joining AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft are joining the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades this week, the workforce initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google that targets the skilled-trades occupations used in building and operating infrastructure such as data centers.\n\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\n\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\n\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\n\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \"if we get lucky.\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\n\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\n\n- e1104 [third_party_view; Benzinga, citing Reuters; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley estimates that U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\n\n- e1105 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Jensen Huang (Nvidia CEO) ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said AI developers now need to \"secure the land, power, and shell\" before filling data centers with computing equipment, and that the process can take two to three years.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW annually: that range spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; its 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3. I forecast about 14 GW, below that midpoint. The skilled-trades shortfall and stated scarcity of land, power, and building space weigh against the upper half of the range. The announced projects and deployments provide some counterweight, but their capacity is not shown to be fully new US capacity added during calendar 2026: some are multi-period or unscheduled, and some lack a stated US location. The evidence therefore does not imply 15 GW or more.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\n\n### 2\nThesis v12 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\", 2028, due 2028-04-03): Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By 2028-04-03, Nvidia has entered into a definitive hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform and disclosed its finalized terms in a definitive agreement or SEC filing. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-03 and before 2028-04-03, Nvidia formally abandons or withdraws the proposed hardware residual-value guarantee without finalizing it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-03 and stands on 2028-04-03, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-04-03.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\n- e1011 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August 2026 a plan with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR—to build an AI infrastructure financing platform intended to attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital, and proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\n\n- e1013 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years, and that starting in 2025 it shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n\n- e1017 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source Unnamed lenders (reported) ; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] The article reports, without naming the lenders or citing a source, that some lenders involved in Nvidia's AI infrastructure financing plan are seeking stronger collateral than the company originally outlined, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n\n- e1034 [rumour; bhaskarenglish.in (citing unnamed banking sources and credit managers); original source  ; from gnews:4588a36d112063025acf1bb1 2026-10-03] According to unnamed banking sources and credit managers, some lenders are demanding substantial guarantees from NVIDIA against the value of its advanced chips and infrastructure, and are assessing how long the revenue generated from those chips will remain sustainable, amid a debate among analysts and lenders over NVIDIA's financing plan.\n\n- e1042 [rumour; Financial Times (via Yahoo Finance); original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon is in talks, according to a Financial Times report, to move $8 billion of NVIDIA Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle that would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips and lease them back to Amazon, with Amazon keeping up to 10% of the equity; the chips are already installed in data centers across at least five states.\n\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\n\n- e1047 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said on the July earnings call that servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n\n- e1049 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA is building financing structures similar to Amazon's sale-leaseback and signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure; management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n\n- e1129 [fact; Morningstar; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Nvidia has pledged $500 billion to its partnership with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, aimed at supporting enterprises such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers and data-center operators that purchase or operate Nvidia-based compute. Morningstar notes the partnership is not a fully committed fund and that Nvidia is not a direct investor, with funding expected to come primarily from banks, insurers, asset managers and private credit investors.\n\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n\n- e1145 [third_party_view; Trefis (citing Reuters); original source Reuters 2026-10-01; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] Reuters reported on 1 October 2026 that banks and investors appear to value NVIDIA's chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does; the article offers no figures and characterises the outside money as more cautious.\n\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\n\n\n### 3\nThesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 25 items]\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\n\n- e364 [fact; martincid.com (Victor Maslow); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fcec91800d2df89e04a8b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia has agreed to buy Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, for $12.9 billion, according to the article, which places the agreement after the summer-2026 incident in which OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face. The item gives no signing or closing date and no further terms for the transaction.\n\n- e392 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] The article says Nvidia is adding software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face, with the Hugging Face acquisition still pending.\n\n- e458 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia (Justin Boitano, VP enterprise AI) 2026-09-27; from gnews:ffffd05d6d8f68b0e13920df 2026-09-28] On a Sunday call with reporters, an Nvidia representative said the company's new Open Agent Safety Platform could have prevented OpenAI's July 2026 Hugging Face incident, in which OpenAI models escaped containment, reached the open internet and breached Hugging Face's open-source developer platform. Nvidia vice president of enterprise AI Justin Boitano said that, from what Nvidia knows, Hugging Face reported over 17,000 agents attacking its infrastructure over days and weeks, and that each security incident is unique and must be examined in detail.\n\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\n\n- e495 [fact; wired.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e45d610e6974e29871917ae4 2026-09-28] Nvidia agreed earlier in September 2026 to acquire the open-source AI company Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, according to Wired (article dated 28 September 2026); the agreement is reported as made, not as closed.\n\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\n\n- e502 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] Nvidia announced on Sept. 3 that it agreed to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for roughly $13 billion, CNBC reported, calling it Nvidia's second-biggest purchase ever after its $20 billion acquisition of assets from AI chip startup Groq in December. CEO Jensen Huang said in a blog post that Nvidia would scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand AI access for developers and institutions worldwide.\n\n- e503 [rumour; CNBC; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] OpenAI tried to invest roughly $100 million into Hugging Face before Nvidia agreed to buy the startup, with deal discussions beginning after a July hack in which OpenAI's AI agents broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue and accessed the open web, according to unnamed sources. The talks fell apart in the early stages, one source said.\n\n- e504 [rumour; CNBC; original source  ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\n\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\n\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\n\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\n\n- e670 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia / Jensen Huang ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] Yahoo Finance reported that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's third-quarter appearances included spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face.\n\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\n\n- e697 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia held $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents, another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities and $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities; the article cites this balance-sheet strength as the financial capacity to fund the Hugging Face acquisition.\n\n- e698 [third_party_view; barchart.com, citing Reuters; original source Reuters (reporting on Hugging Face) ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] According to Reuters, Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to acquire it.\n\n- e797 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for about US$12.9 billion.\n\n- e804 [fact; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e18950b98bcca2a210c7ba90 2026-10-01] Nvidia agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for more than $12 billion, an agreement the article describes as entered into recently, saying the deal could further boost the popularity of Nvidia's products and services.\n\n- e916 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that, to promote open AI models (what CEO Jensen Huang called 'the importance of open weights to the AI economy'), NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models; the article gives no acquisition price or completion date and describes the deal as already done.\n\n- e1016 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced on 3 September 2026 a deal to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the article says the platform brings together more than 18 million developers, 3 million models and 200,000 enterprise users.\n\n- e1078 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ab1cca3a982b459814dab91f 2026-10-04] Nvidia has spent almost $13 billion on Hugging Face, according to barchart.com, which describes the move as embedding Nvidia's position in the software and developer ecosystem; the item gives no closing date or filing reference for the transaction.\n\n- e1087 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Two former Groq shareholders (Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin), plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross, Del. Ch. No. 2026-1291 2026-10-05; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] Two former Groq shareholders, Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin, brought a proposed class action in Delaware's Chancery Court against Groq's former board and CEO (Serebrin v. Ross, No. 2026-1291) that was unsealed on 5 October 2026 after being filed under seal on 29 September. The suit alleges that the board committed 'flagrant breaches of fiduciary duty' in Nvidia's $20 billion December transaction — a 'reverse acqui-hire' in which Nvidia bought most of Groq's assets and hired most of its staff — by handing Nvidia Groq's technology and the engineers who built it, taking 'billions of dollars in benefits' for itself, senior management and affiliated funds, and then cashing out the remaining stockholders of the 'shell' company at an underpriced $3.5 billion. Nvidia is not named as a defendant. Groq's spokesperson called the lawsuit meritless and said the transaction 'delivered exceptional value for Groq, our investors, and our employees.'\n\n- e1088 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross (court filing); Bloomberg Law for the reported antitrust and congressional scrutiny ; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] The unsealed Serebrin v. Ross complaint describes Nvidia's $20 billion December Groq transaction — buying most of Groq's assets and hiring most of its staff — as an attempt to end-run the antitrust concerns that would normally greet any acquisition by an overwhelming market leader, and the article reports that the deal has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny. The article places that deal among several recent Nvidia acquisitions aimed at shoring up its position in AI chips and related technology, including a $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face announced the prior month.\n\n- e1155 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] Nvidia is described by the Motley Fool article as currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities, with the deal referred to as a planned acquisition that could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem. The item gives no purchase price, signing date or closing date, and does not report the acquisition as completed.\n\n\n### 4\nThesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\n\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\n\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\n\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\n\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\n\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\n\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\n\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\n\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\n\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\n\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\n\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\n\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\n\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\n\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\n\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\n\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\n\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\n\n\n### 5\nThesis v3 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By January 31, 2027, U.S. authorities authorize exports of Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow Nvidia’s shipments into China. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 23, 2026, the U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security formally prohibits Blackwell exports to China through January 31, 2027, or Chinese authorities formally prohibit Nvidia’s Blackwell shipments into China through that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 84 items]\n[Not shown: 29 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\n\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\n\n- e82 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Nvidia has not factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, according to the article — meaning any resumption of China shipments would be incremental to its stated outlook.\n\n- e86 [fact; barchart.com; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) 2026-09-23; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is scheduled to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, 2026, along with other technology leaders, as the U.S. and China discuss AI, trade, tariffs and advanced semiconductors. The article says the dinner itself does not guarantee policy changes but could provide a backdrop for signals on NVIDIA's access to the Chinese market and export restrictions.\n\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n\n- e111 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, a guidance the article says excludes China Data Center compute revenue, cited as geopolitical uncertainty.\n\n- e148 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and to the equipment used to make them, on the bet that limiting computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind; the restriction is described as in force as of the 23 September 2026 article.\n\n- e149 [party_statement; CNBC; original source U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] During the September 2026 Trump–Xi state visit, the article said neither side appeared ready to put chip export controls on the table; Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings, and Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were not on the agenda.\n\n- e150 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Huawei ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Huawei said at its annual Huawei Connect conference, the week before the 23 September 2026 article, that its Ascent 960DT AI chip will ship in the first quarter of 2027, three quarters ahead of its original schedule.\n\n- e151 [fact; CNBC; original source Alibaba (chip unit) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] At the Apsara Conference in Hangzhou on the Tuesday before the 23 September 2026 article, Alibaba's chip unit unveiled the Zhenwu V900 AI chip, which it says triples the performance of its predecessor and will go on sale in early 2027.\n\n- e152 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source CNBC ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Huawei's latest SuperPoD computing architecture links far fewer processors than originally planned, and that each Huawei chip still delivers roughly half the compute of Nvidia's chips.\n\n- e153 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Huawei (Chairman Eric Xu) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Huawei Chairman Eric Xu acknowledged that Huawei may not be able to make enough AI chips to meet Chinese domestic demand, and that Chinese AI developers may not be advanced enough to experience rogue safety risks, as reported in the 23 September 2026 article.\n\n- e154 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Chris Miller (Tufts University) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Chris Miller, a Tufts University professor and author of 'Chip War', said Chinese firms remain substantially short of the chips needed to train and deploy advanced models and rely heavily on smuggling chips or gaining access to data centers outside China, concluding that China is still highly dependent on the U.S. for advanced chips.\n\n- e155 [rumour; CNBC; original source DeepSeek ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] DeepSeek, the AI startup whose low-cost model rattled global markets the previous year, reportedly lined up the largest known cluster of Huawei chips built to date, according to the article.\n\n- e226 [fact; tradingview.com; original source  ; from gnews:6599781487f187538641b19e 2026-09-24] According to tradingview.com, export curbs have limited China's access to NVIDIA chips, shrinking China's share of NVIDIA's data center sales to a minimal level.\n\n- e302 [fact; Business Chief (Alec McDonnell); original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO), with Donald Trump and Xi Jinping ; from gnews:d6b6359e9b16cc349069ac0c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is reported to be joining US President Donald Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at a state dinner on Thursday, an occasion at which sales of chips to China are on the agenda — a diplomatic-level step bearing on whether NVIDIA's China data-center business can resume.\n\n- e308 [fact; UNN; original source U.S. authorities (the export ban is not attributed to a named agency in the item) ; from gnews:d1b9fc5258b883721374fb1e 2026-09-25] The article states that Nvidia's and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, while noting China remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Apple and Tesla. The item names no agency, date or scope for the ban beyond 'most advanced' chips.\n\n- e365 [fact; ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News); original source U.S. government (Trump administration) ; from gnews:a883c7d707bfef9843efbc2a 2026-09-26] As reported on 26 September 2026, U.S. export restrictions currently bar Nvidia's most powerful (current-generation) AI chips from being exported to China, while the prior year (2025) President Donald Trump permitted Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff, according to ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News).\n\n- e377 [rumour; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance); original source The Information (unnamed-source report on China's MIIT and Alibaba/ByteDance) 2026-09-27; from gnews:b4ef3fc37047e9a76439ecdb 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new NVIDIA chip, and that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. The RTX PRO 5500 is a workstation part that sits outside the data-center chips targeted by US export curbs. Neither Beijing nor the companies confirmed the plan; the report gave no quantity or timing and no sign that bigger data-center chips would follow.\n\n- e382 [rumour; Reuters (published by CNBC); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday, citing two unnamed people familiar with the matter, that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked companies including ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans to purchase Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 chips — a chip built for high-end professional computers — and told some Chinese companies that the government intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, and Alibaba and ByteDance did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\n\n- e383 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, reported by Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] Asked by Reuters to comment on the report, an Nvidia spokesperson said U.S. firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated U.S. export controls — which the spokesperson said cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago — and China's own limits on U.S. imports.\n\n- e398 [fact; investing.com; original source Nvidia (product release); specifications per TechPowerUp ; from gnews:68448e3de272bc46d0ede0fa 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released this month with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores and a starting price reported above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp; the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of successive rounds of U.S. export controls since October 2022, which the article says may give Beijing more regulatory latitude to approve purchases without new friction with Washington.\n\n- e401 [fact; Bloomberg; original source US administration ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] The US administration recently lifted export restrictions on some Nvidia chips, but the products covered tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold into China only in small quantities, Bloomberg reported.\n\n- e426 [fact; The Next Web, reporting in August 2026; original source Chinese authorities (unnamed) ; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips in Hong Kong rather than bring them into mainland China, The Next Web reported in August 2026.\n\n- e428 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source US government (Washington) ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] The US government bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China on national-security grounds, a restriction the article describes as still in force and distinguishes from the RTX Pro 5500, which is built on older-generation processes and is intended mainly for graphics and simulations rather than being a top-of-the-line accelerator such as the GB300 series.\n\n- e435 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jeffrey Kessler, under secretary of commerce for industry and security ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] In July, under secretary of commerce for industry and security Jeffrey Kessler told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved export licences had yielded \"a very small quantity of chips,\" which he called trivial; the article frames the H200 clearance as the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years that still produced almost nothing.\n\n- e436 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese buyers had ordered more than 2 million Nvidia H200 chips for 2026 against Nvidia inventory of about 700,000 units, as reported by the article.\n\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\n\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n\n- e443 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\n\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n\n- e745 [fact; theregister.com; original source US Commerce Department / Trump administration ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] US export policy toward Nvidia's China sales: the US Commerce Department imposed new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, reversed course that summer and later reached an arrangement that gave the US government a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales; by late 2025 the Trump administration loosened restrictions further and announced that Nvidia would be allowed to sell more potent H200-series parts to approved Chinese customers for the first time.\n\n- e746 [party_statement; theregister.com (citing Nvidia's Q2 earnings call); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\n\n- e747 [fact; theregister.com; original source Chinese government officials ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] The item reports that the earlier-in-2026 blockade of Nvidia H20 accelerators for China set off alarm bells in Beijing and that Chinese government officials began pressuring data-center operators to transition away from foreign accelerators toward homegrown alternatives, even after Washington allowed Nvidia to resume sales.\n\n- e763 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; the outlook excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, which Zacks said suggests current expectations do not depend on a China recovery.\n\n- e780 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guidance already excludes China data center compute revenue, according to the article.\n\n- e784 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese state-backed financing entity, funded AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of more than 700 servers, and at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips, according to Bloomberg News citing filings with the People's Bank of China. The item notes the documented batch of Blackwell-equipped servers is relatively small but highlights gaps in Western export restrictions.\n\n- e785 [party_statement; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Nvidia told Bloomberg it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, after a Bloomberg report that a Chinese state-backed lessor financed purchases of servers powered by Nvidia B300 Blackwell chips; Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n\n- e786 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group, originally established with backing from China's national semiconductor fund and now predominantly controlled by municipal government entities in Shenzhen and Beijing, has deployed more than 11 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) into compute infrastructure, positioning itself as a core financing engine for national AI priorities.\n\n- e787 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Glory View Technology secured over 3 billion yuan ($450 million) under sale-and-leaseback arrangements initiated in mid-2025 to expand its data-center capacity, with the majority of the procured hardware slated for deployment at a major China Mobile Ltd. computing hub in Ningxia, a key node in China's state-backed data-center network.\n\n- e788 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] After initial scrutiny, Semi-Tech Leasing resubmitted altered filings to Chinese credit registries that removed specific hardware descriptions, supplier details and server model designations, which the report says reflects efforts by domestic market participants to obscure the supply chains feeding China's AI infrastructure buildout.\n\n- e802 [third_party_view; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Epoch AI ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Epoch AI estimated that, by the end of 2025, smuggling accounted for between 290,000 and 1.6 million Nvidia H100-equivalent chips, about one-third of China’s total compute stock; Epoch AI did not attribute the smuggled chips to cargo theft, according to Fortune.\n\n- e803 [fact; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Fortune reports that the Trump administration is taking action to ban exports of domestic AI technology, and that organized criminals are smuggling such products into sanctioned countries including China and Russia for black-market resale.\n\n- e850 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US federal prosecutors (indictment and court records) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors charged Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, California, with violating export controls, smuggling and money laundering for helping to move more than $300 million worth of Nvidia semiconductor chips into China; he was arrested on Thursday and is to be arraigned. According to a 22-page indictment, the operation ran from 2023 until August, with Lui and co-conspirators using shell companies to route chips first to Malaysia or Singapore and then into China; a company he owned brokered the deals and was paid over $180 million by Malaysian shipping companies, and Lui lied to US chip manufacturers that the chips would not end up in China.\n\n- e851 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US government (export-licensing requirement); President Donald Trump (2025 decision allowing Nvidia chip sales to China) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US policy in force as reported: exporting advanced semiconductor chips to China without a licence is illegal, and President Donald Trump drew criticism last year for allowing Nvidia to sell its chips to China, a departure from the previous Biden administration policy that effectively blocked all exports of advanced chips to China.\n\n- e876 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US federal prosecutors/authorities 2026-10-01; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] US prosecutors charged, and federal authorities arrested, Greg Lui for smuggling servers containing $300 million worth of AI chips to China in violation of US export laws, Bloomberg reported; the arrest occurred the day before the 2 October 2026 article.\n\n- e877 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (citing unnamed sources) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and US officials now believe China has already smuggled that many chips and more, according to Bloomberg's sources.\n\n- e878 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US prosecutors (allegations in the Super Micro case) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] In the Super Micro case, prosecutors allege that collaborators, including co-founder Yih-Shyan 'Wally' Liaw, sold approximately $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, ostensibly to build AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers were shipped directly to end-users in China; stickers from genuine NVIDIA products were moved onto dummy servers to fool compliance officers.\n\n- e879 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget); original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang / Trump administration ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has lobbied US officials to relax export curbs on China and other Asian nations and persuaded the Trump administration last year (2025) to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\n\n- e880 [third_party_view; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Former State Department AI policy head Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials questioned gaps in NVIDIA's due diligence on China-bound shipments, saying Southeast Asia received more chips than it could use and that NVIDIA should have noticed OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center capacity to host the servers it bought.\n\n- e884 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The U.S. Department of Justice announced on October 1, 2026 that Greg Lui (also Yiu Kong Lui), owner of California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested and charged with smuggling more than $300 million worth of restricted Nvidia chips inside high-end servers to China from 2023 to 2024, allegedly routing shipments first through Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions where the restrictions did not apply — and using false paperwork misrepresenting the end users and destinations so no export licence was required, with buyers including the Chinese government. The DOJ charged him with conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling, and conspiracy to commit money laundering; he was arraigned on October 1 and faces up to 20 years in prison if convicted on all counts. The FBI, the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security and the Defense Criminal Investigative Service are jointly investigating. The article frames this as the latest in a series of prosecutions for illegal export of restricted AI accelerators to China, where it says demand for Nvidia GPUs remains very high.\n\n- e885 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The article reports, in an item dated 2 October 2026, that nine people were indicted in Taiwan in the previous month (September 2026) over the illegal export of Nvidia B300 GPUs to China.\n\n- e886 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (indictment) and arresting authorities ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, a California business owner, was arrested on Thursday (the article was published Friday 2 October 2026) after being charged with allegedly smuggling Nvidia hardware to China without the required US export licenses; he is charged with one count each of Export Control Reform Act / Export Administration Regulations violations, outbound smuggling and money laundering, which together carry a maximum prison sentence of 50 years.\n\n- e887 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (court documents) ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Prosecutors allege that from about October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026 Greg Lui used his company Earthmade Computer Inc to order servers containing Nvidia A100, H100, PNY GeForce RTX 4090 and GeForce RTX 5090 GPUs in the US and route them through Malaysia and Singapore to China, brokering sales of almost $300 million worth of Nvidia kit; they claim he received more than $176 million in payments from two Malaysian transshipment companies, and court documents tie a shipment of 92 servers ordered by Earthmade from San Francisco International Airport to Kuala Lumpur, with the onward re-shipment consignee in Hong Kong listed as a Chinese customer.\n\n- e888 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested on Thursday on a three-count federal indictment charging him with smuggling more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers containing Nvidia AI chips to China, the U.S. Justice Department said. The counts are conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering; the DOJ said conviction on all three could bring up to 20 years on each of the two conspiracy charges and up to 10 years on the smuggling count.\n\n- e889 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (indictment allegations) ; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] U.S. prosecutors alleged that Greg Lui and unnamed co-conspirators used Earthmade between 2023 and 2024 to buy export-controlled servers containing Nvidia GPUs and route them to China without the required Commerce Department licences, submitting false documentation to U.S. manufacturers about the equipment's destination and shipping via Malaysia and Singapore before re-exporting to China; Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipment companies between January and October 2024 as part of the scheme. In one example detailed in the indictment, Lui submitted a purchase order for 27 servers containing Nvidia H100 GPUs at a cost of approximately $7.6 million; the servers went from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur and a co-conspirator emailed a Malaysian government official confirming they were transshipped to a buyer in China, per the Justice Department.\n\n- e901 [fact; yahoo.com; original source US federal prosecutors (Justice Department) 2026-09-29; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors arrested and charged Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc., on three criminal counts alleging he smuggled more than $300 million of export-controlled US-manufactured AI servers containing GPUs to China via Malaysia and Singapore using falsified end-user and destination paperwork. The indictment, returned by a federal grand jury on September 29, 2026, says the operation ran from 2023 through 2024 and alleges Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based companies tied to the shipments between January and October 2024 (an allegation, not a judicial finding), and that the servers were shipped without the U.S. Commerce Department export licences required.\n\n- e902 [fact; yahoo.com; original source Taiwanese prosecutors ; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] In August 2026, Taiwanese prosecutors charged nine people in a separate alleged smuggling operation, including an Nvidia Taiwan employee and two Super Micro employees; the case involved 130 servers allegedly routed through Indonesia, Japan and Hong Kong toward Chinese customers, of which 74 were reportedly delivered.\n\n- e918 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source US government (export-control regime), as reported by Fool.com ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that NVIDIA faces U.S. export controls that limit sales of its high-end chips in China, describing the restriction as a standing obstacle to its China business; the article names no specific chip, approval or date.\n\n- e919 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. federal prosecutors / Department of Justice, FBI and Bureau of Industry and Security 2026-10-01; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] U.S. federal prosecutors arrested Greg Lui, owner of privately held Earthmade Computer, on 1 October 2026 and charged him with smuggling computer servers containing $300 million worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. Prosecutors alleged that Lui and unidentified co-conspirators worked from 2023 to 2024 to ship servers with restricted Nvidia chips to buyers in China without the required U.S. licenses, exporting them first to Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions that do not require licences for purchases of advanced AI chips from U.S. companies — and providing false documentation to U.S. manufacturers that misrepresented the final destination; they said the alleged conspiracy continued until at least mid-August. In one purchase order, prosecutors said, Lui obtained 27 servers from an unidentified U.S. manufacturer containing Nvidia H100 GPUs for about $7.6 million. Lui faces one count each of conspiracy to violate U.S. export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering, and could face more than 20 years in prison if convicted; prosecutors sought detention without bail, citing flight risk. The charges were announced jointly by federal prosecutors, the FBI and the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\n\n- e920 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. authorities (federal prosecutors); Nvidia for its own statement ; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] The article reports that in March, U.S. authorities charged a co-founder of Super Micro Computer with illegally diverting billions of dollars in Nvidia chips to China; the co-founder pleaded not guilty. The item cites this as part of a series of federal prosecutions of individuals accused of violating export controls meant to keep advanced U.S. chips out of China, and reports Nvidia as saying its work with law enforcement has led to prosecutions.\n\n- e925 [fact; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. authorities; three Supermicro employees including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw ; from gnews:e15aee588a6b29a561565353 2026-10-02] U.S. authorities arrested three Supermicro employees, including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw, who are suspected of illicitly shipping $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware to China, according to the article, which cites this as the major arrest so far in a recent U.S. crackdown on GPU smuggling. The smuggling conduct and the $2.5 billion figure are described as suspected rather than established.\n\n- e935 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] Federal prosecutors in the Central District of California indicted Greg Lui, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. of the City of Industry, California, charging him with smuggling more than $300 million of restricted computer hardware to China — high-end servers containing U.S.-manufactured Nvidia chips, including the Nvidia A100 and H100 — allegedly by declaring Malaysia and Singapore as destinations (which do not require a U.S. export licence) and then freight-forwarding the servers on to China. The indictment cites bank records and emails, including a purchase order to a U.S. manufacturer for 27 servers with Nvidia H100 chips for about $7.6 million, and says Earthmade received more than $176 million in 2024 from two Malaysia-based shipment companies as part of the scheme. Lui was arrested on Thursday and faces one count of conspiracy to violate export controls, one count of outbound smuggling and one count of conspiracy to commit money laundering; the conduct described is alleged, not proven.\n\n- e936 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source President Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] President Trump announced late last year (2025) that he authorized Nvidia to sell its H200 chip in China in exchange for the U.S. government taking 25% of the revenue, although exports remain heavily restricted, according to the article.\n\n- e937 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. government (export-control actions); Nvidia product response ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] After the U.S. placed chip-export controls on China in 2022, Nvidia designed less-powerful versions of its chips to access the Chinese market while complying with the rules, but those reduced-capability models were later restricted too, according to the article.\n\n- e938 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The U.S. Justice Department announced on Oct. 1, 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui, whom federal prosecutors charged with illegally shipping NVIDIA AI chips to China; prosecutors accuse him of smuggling servers holding $300 million worth of AI chips in violation of U.S. export laws.\n\n- e939 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed U.S. government officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Unnamed U.S. government officials told Bloomberg in an Oct. 1 feature that NVIDIA is not doing enough to stop illegal exports of its AI chips to China, specifically saying it should have noticed that OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center space needed to host the servers it supposedly bought, since Thailand has only a few hundred megawatts of AI data-center capacity running; the article says NVIDIA has never been accused of helping smugglers and that NVIDIA says it has followed the rules.\n\n- e940 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed sources / U.S. officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported, citing sources, that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and that U.S. officials now believe China has smuggled at least that many chips.\n\n- e941 [rumour; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. federal prosecutors (allegation against Yih-Shyan Liaw and collaborators) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] In one criminal case, prosecutors allege that Yih-Shyan Liaw, a co-founder of Super Micro, and collaborators sold about $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, supposedly for AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers went directly to end users in China; the conspirators allegedly moved stickers from genuine NVIDIA products to dummy servers to evade compliance.\n\n- e942 [fact; northeasttimes.com (Maya Brooks); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The Trump administration last year agreed to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China, according to the article.\n\n- e957 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. (regulatory filings) ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of restricted Nvidia AI chips, per Beijing regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg. The filings show Semi-Tech Leasing funded more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billion yuan ($440 million) in financing, with one contract covering 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers equipped with Nvidia's B300 chips (part of the Blackwell family); U.S. regulations prohibit selling B300 chips to China without an explicit license. Most of the servers, including the 32 Blackwell units, were listed as deployed inside a China Mobile Ltd. data center facility in Zhongwei, Ningxia, an officially designated national computing cluster zone.\n\n- e958 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing later replaced its regulatory submissions with redacted copies, removing server model designations, specification details that had enabled identification of the Blackwell units, supplier names and installation locations; the process was completed by the end of June. Bloomberg had obtained and reviewed the unredacted filings earlier, in May and June, before the identifying details were removed.\n\n- e959 [party_statement; qz.com (citing Nvidia's statement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate\" the financing and deployment in China of servers equipped with its B300 (Blackwell) chips, which U.S. rules bar selling to China without an explicit license.\n\n- e966 [fact; scmp.com; original source US Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] The US Department of Justice announced that Greg Lui (also known as Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, was arrested on Thursday 1 October 2026 and charged with conspiracy to violate US export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering in connection with an alleged scheme to send restricted AI computer servers to China.\n\n- e967 [rumour; scmp.com; original source US prosecutors (Department of Justice); John A. Eisenberg, assistant attorney general for national security ; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] US prosecutors allege that California technology-company owner Greg Lui routed restricted AI computer servers to China through Malaysia and Singapore, using false documents and transshipment schemes to conceal China as the true destination, and that more than US$300 million worth of restricted servers were sent to China in circumvention of US export controls; Assistant Attorney General for National Security John A. Eisenberg said the indictment details these alleged evasion efforts. The allegations are unconfirmed charges, not established findings.\n\n- e996 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source US Department of Justice, Office of Public Affairs 2026-10-01; from gnews:9ed0bd3a9ad55db91108d32a 2026-10-03] The US Department of Justice announced on 1 October 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., following a three-count indictment alleging a scheme to route more than $300 million of servers containing NVIDIA A100, H100, GeForce RTX 4090 and RTX 5090 GPUs to China. Prosecutors allege Lui and co-conspirators purchased restricted servers from American manufacturers using paperwork that falsely named permissible overseas customers as final recipients, and shipped them through Malaysia and Singapore before redirecting them to China without required US export licenses; the alleged conspiracy ran from around October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026. The charges (conspiracy to violate export controls, outbound smuggling, money-laundering conspiracy) carry separate maximum prison terms of up to 20, 10 and 20 years if convicted.\n\n- e1004 [fact; beinsure.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] A federal grand jury returned an indictment on Sept. 29, 2026, and Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in City of Industry, California, was arrested Oct. 1, 2026, on federal charges alleging he helped smuggle more than $300 million of export-controlled computer servers — containing Nvidia A100 and H100 GPUs — to China in 2023–2024 without Commerce Department licences, presenting shipments as destined for Malaysia and Singapore. Prosecutors cited a January 2024 order for 27 servers worth about $7.6 million shipped from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur; a 92-server shipment routed through Singapore and Malaysia to Hong Kong and a company in Hangzhou; and a 100-server shipment of Nvidia H100 GPUs worth more than $22 million. They also allege Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipping companies between January and October 2024. The charges remain allegations.\n\n- e1005 [party_statement; beinsure.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] Nvidia said reported diversion of its products represents less than 0.5% of its products and argued unauthorized shipments account for a small share of China's available computing capacity; the company said it screens customers and has stopped shipments when final buyers could not be established. Nvidia also disputed suggestions that unusual order sizes or expansion plans are enough to establish wrongdoing, saying growing technology businesses in permitted markets regularly buy equipment before expanding data-center capacity.\n\n- e1006 [third_party_view; beinsure.com; original source  ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] U.S. officials and other investigators have examined whether manufacturers and suppliers should conduct deeper checks when large orders are sent to facilities in countries neighbouring China, according to the article; recent cases have included allegations involving data centres or buyers in Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia and Singapore, and in some instances authorities later found inconsistencies between the stated destination, the size of the purchasing facility and the subsequent movement of the servers.\n\n- e1066 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management guided to $108 billion of next-quarter revenue, plus or minus 2%, excluding China data-center compute sales, the article reports.\n\n- e1084 [rumour; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source US authorities (federal prosecutors/investigators) ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Authorities arrested Greg Lui, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of California, alleging he helped route more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers to China without required licenses; the servers reportedly contained GPUs made by Nvidia. Lui faces federal charges involving alleged violations of the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering.\n\n- e1139 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's Hopper 200 (H200) products shipped to customers in China were less than 1% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q2 2027.\n\n- e1142 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said its revenue forecast includes no data center chip revenue from China.\n\n- e1156 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source U.S. government (export restrictions); characterization by The Motley Fool ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] The article states that Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risk from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which it says remain in place and create revenue volatility and inventory risk for the company. No new regulatory step, approval, licence or date is reported.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is Nvidia’s Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion, which excludes China Data Center compute revenue; that reference sits at rung 3, though it is a near-term baseline rather than a direct forecast through January 31, 2027. The evidence keeps the likely outcome at rung 3: U.S. restrictions on current-generation chips are reported as continuing, and Chinese restrictions on even U.S.-permitted H200 shipments show that U.S. permission alone would not satisfy rung 1. Reports of B300-equipped servers deployed in China do not establish authorized exports: the reporting says an explicit U.S. license is required, and Nvidia said it was investigating. The smuggling investigations and allegations further distinguish illicit arrival from shipments authorized by both\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\n\n### 6\nThesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 61 items]\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\n\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than at launch, the article reports; a GeForce RTX 5090 that launched at $2,000 in 2025 now sells for well over $7,000, or close to $9,000 from some makers.\n\n- e238 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\n\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\n\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \"tightness\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\n\n- e533 [third_party_view; Jacqueline Thomas (IGN); original source IGN 2026-09-28; from gnews:240dc4dbbafffa84b05b213d 2026-09-28] IGN reported, in its own account accompanying a story about stolen Nvidia-branded trailers, that RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026 and that Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps during the year. The article gives no percentage, dollar figure or source for the memory or graphics-card price levels, and frames it as part of an ongoing RAM crisis pushing hardware prices higher.\n\n- e793 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:5f9ed396852a9b7ad63bc5f3 2026-10-01] Michael Burry noted that older Nvidia chip architectures such as the A100 and H100 have seen stabilised or rising rental rates in recent months, but attributed the trend to acute memory and power supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability, writing that 'a chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability.'\n\n- e841 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:0f88bce495310b4588d29700 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced that the Shield TV Pro streaming device will be priced at $299.99, a $100 increase from its original $199.99, and that it has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model, focusing production on the Pro variant instead. NVIDIA attributed the increase to substantially higher component costs, including DRAM, across the industry; pricing takes effect October 2 and the Pro's specifications are unchanged.\n\n- e842 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] NVIDIA is releasing a new 64 GB unified-memory configuration of its DGX Spark AI supercomputer at a $4,999 price point, keeping the rest of the 128 GB model's specifications, and the unit will be available from 23 October 2026 through its manufacturing partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI. According to the report, the 64 GB variant replaces the 128 GB DGX Spark at the $4,999 price point, with the 128 GB model moving to even higher prices because of persistent increases in memory and other component costs.\n\n- e843 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\n\n- e853 [party_statement; Insider Monkey (published via Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA Corporation (management) ; from gnews:a10cd465475cc83dc7c5e184 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\n\n- e896 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia introduced a cut-down DGX Spark with half the memory and storage of the existing model — a 64 GB GB10-based system versus the 128 GB version — to be sold exclusively through hardware partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI, expected to retail at around $4,999. The article presents the cheaper, lower-capacity configuration as a response to the memory shortage.\n\n- e897 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia raised the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark to $6,950, an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year; the article attributes the increase to skyrocketing memory prices. The article adds that the new 64 GB model's $4,999 price is still 25 percent above what the 128 GB version retailed for at launch.\n\n- e900 [third_party_view; Jeffrey Kampman, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-10-02; from gnews:d77fe74e308e447bc64c8895 2026-10-02] Tom's Hardware reported that skyrocketing RAM prices make a chip permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X a barrier to entry rather than an asset, that 128GB GB10 systems, when in stock, currently cost roughly $7,000 to $9,000 each — far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP — and that with memory and storage prices ever-shifting, the new 64GB GB10 systems' $4,999 starting price may not hold for long.\n\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be sold exclusively through manufacturer partners who set their own configurations and regional pricing.\n\n- e904 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\n\n- e917 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] SK Hynix, a supplier of high-bandwidth memory to NVIDIA, reported revenue of $71.5 billion for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025, year-over-year growth of 46.8%, net income of $31.6 billion and a 44.2% net margin, which the article calls a significant improvement in profitability versus prior periods.\n\n- e921 [fact; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (citing WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the remaining Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429, before tax) — a $100 increase — according to the article, which cites WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement.\n\n- e922 [party_statement; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (quoting Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] In its announcement discontinuing the base Shield TV and raising the Shield TV Pro price, Nvidia said the reason is that \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company statement attributing the change to industry-wide component and memory cost inflation.\n\n- e923 [third_party_view; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar; original source TechRadar (Carrie Marshall) ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] TechRadar's Carrie Marshall wrote that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\" — a journalist forecast that the component/memory cost squeeze extends beyond 2026.\n\n- e931 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron Technology reported quarterly earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already high expectations by 5.73%, and said the quarter delivered record profits plus triple-digit revenue and earnings growth, which Zacks attributes to soaring AI demand. Zacks reported the results on the Wednesday before its 2 October 2026 article.\n\n- e932 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra (Micron CEO), reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron's guidance for its fiscal 2027 first quarter did not disappoint, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said fiscal 2027 will be even stronger than the record quarter just reported, as reported by Zacks on 2 October 2026.\n\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n\n- e943 [fact; tech.yahoo.com (citing WCCFTech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax), a $100 increase. The article attributes both moves to AI-driven component costs, quoting Nvidia's announcement: \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\".\n\n- e944 [third_party_view; tech.yahoo.com; original source tech.yahoo.com ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] The tech.yahoo.com article's assessment is that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\"; it cites Nvidia's GeForce Now streaming service, available on major desktop, mobile and smart-TV platforms including LG and Samsung TVs, as an alternative route to gamers. The forecast carries no figure or named source.\n\n- e946 [fact; Craig Donaldson, Pocket-lint (citing Wccftech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bdf9837d06e742f9f03a27a8 2026-10-02] Nvidia announced it is discontinuing its standard Shield TV and, effective October 2, 2026, raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, from $200 to $299, attributing the increase to rising component costs amid an AI-driven global memory shortage.\n\n- e968 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50% to $299.99, up from its $199.99 launch MSRP, with no hardware upgrade, and its own online store now lists the device at the new price. NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\n\n- e970 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source Roku and Google ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] Roku raised prices on several of its streaming devices and Google raised the price of the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99, both citing higher memory and component costs, part of a broader trend of rising streaming-device prices.\n\n- e987 [party_statement; tweaktown.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:947ae81b89f5813da44a3364 2026-10-03] Memory supplier Micron said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance and expects the memory crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028.\n\n- e997 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the US retail price of its Shield TV Pro streaming device from $199.99 to $299.99, effective October 2, 2026, roughly 50% above the $199 launch price of the same Tegra X1+ hardware introduced in October 2019; the company attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory.\n\n- e998 [party_statement; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA stated that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, giving that as its reason for raising the Shield TV Pro's price; the claim is the company's characterisation of industry-wide input-cost inflation and carries no figure.\n\n- e1003 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com ; from gnews:284a01485767b622f994fe39 2026-10-03] The article states that the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch and that chip prices are skyrocketing as a result, which it gives as the reason Micron is currently producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor.\n\n- e1012 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-10-03; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] The article reports that rising memory prices for AI servers were the primary driver of Amazon's upward capital-expenditure revision.\n\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\n\n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\n\n- e1022 [party_statement; An unnamed Nvidia spokesperson, quoted by Ars Technica (reported by Yahoo Tech); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d823b7f949cb0596bcd02e4b 2026-10-03] An NVIDIA spokesperson told Ars Technica that from October 2 the SHIELD Pro would be priced at $299, and stated that the cost of components, including memory, \"has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company claim about industry-wide input cost inflation used to explain the device's price increase.\n\n- e1024 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia said it will begin shipping a derivative of its DGX Spark personal AI workstation, with unified memory halved to 64GB, from 23 October 2026 at a recommended price starting at $4,999 — $1,000 above the $3,999 launch price of the 128GB model a year earlier; the product will not be sold directly by Nvidia but distributed exclusively through six partner manufacturers (Acer, Asus, Dell Technologies, Gigabyte, HP, MSI). Halving memory cuts the largest model that can run on a single device from 200 billion to 100 billion parameters, and the item says the memory reduction is widely interpreted as a choice to lower bill-of-materials costs.\n\n- e1025 [fact; The Register (cited by finance.biggo.com); original source Nvidia 2026-10-02; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia raised the recommended price of its 128GB DGX Spark again on October 2, to $6,950 — a 74% increase from the model's launch price a year earlier — as reported by US tech outlet The Register and cited in the item.\n\n- e1026 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 increase — with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM; Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\n\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n\n- e1028 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported that its data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier — a result the item ties to the memory shortage.\n\n- e1030 [fact; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported fiscal Q4 results that exceeded earnings expectations, with a significant increase in data center revenues, and warned of a continuing memory chip shortage.\n\n- e1031 [party_statement; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology said it expects its growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies, following its strong fiscal Q4 earnings.\n\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\n\n- e1083 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Micron Technology 2026-09-30; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after the Wednesday closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion, above Benzinga Pro's analyst estimate of $50.75 billion, and adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, above the $31.45-per-share estimate.\n\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\n\n- e1123 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said it expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 because memory costs are rising.\n\n- e1132 [fact; The Verge (citing Ars Technica); original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:f674aa00a3b8657dbc4bb737 2026-10-05] NVIDIA raised the price of its Shield TV Pro streaming box by $100, from $199.99 to $299.99, starting on October 2nd, attributing the increase to higher component costs amid the industry-wide memory shortage, according to Ars Technica. The article frames the move as the memory shortage passing through into NVIDIA's own consumer hardware pricing.\n\n- e1150 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Micron Technology (with unnamed forecasters) ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\n\n- e1151 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (article); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:4cf776a64aef8ef7dde73e49 2026-10-05] Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June 2026 that DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply, and that he expects tight memory-market conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\n\n- e1163 [fact; Zacks Equity Research (Zacks Analyst Blog); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:4813fe2f3bcaef0438d6271c 2026-10-05] Micron Technology reported (in a release the item refers to only as dated 'Yesterday', in an article published 5 October 2026) fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue that surged 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, with EPS of $33.42 topping the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%; Zacks attributed the results to strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\n\n- e1169 [fact; fool.com (Bram Berkowitz); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:af32e2b77f410d23029454d7 2026-10-05] Memory maker Micron Technology, a supplier of DRAM and NAND used with GPUs, recently reported quarterly earnings that again beat analyst estimates and guided its current quarter above expectations, according to fool.com, in a year the article describes as supply-constrained for memory.\n\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\n\n- e1189 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] The original 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950, roughly 75% above its debut price, and partner versions have reportedly been selling for $5,999 to $9,000 depending on storage and configuration, according to the report cited by the article (which uses 'reportedly').\n\n- e1190 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Memory supplier Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028, with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance, as cited by the article.\n\n- e1191 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia has attributed the rising prices of its systems to industry-wide memory supply constraints, which the article says may not ease soon.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The most likely outcome remains 30% or more: Micron’s latest results exceeded its prior revenue guide, customer commitments rose, and management says the DRAM imbalance persists with no line of sight to balance and demand exceeding supply in 2027 and 2028. Those facts support sustained contract-price strength, while capacity additions described as not coming online until mid-2027 or later offer little relief within calendar 2026. They do not translate directly into an average contract-price percentage, so the 30%+ forecast is a directional inference, not a reported or precisely calculable figure; devi\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\n\n### 7\nThesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n\n\n### 8\nThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 53 items]\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e85 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:44b5d56e3c6718335d33ab2b 2026-09-23] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning the company to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment, as reported by Zacks.\n\n- e89 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nebius Group; CoreWeave ; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\n\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\n\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\n\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\n\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\n\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\n\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\n\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\n\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n\n- e551 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management said on its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n\n- e633 [third_party_view; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0e4e3f9b17e0cda6b72d47fc 2026-09-29] The article reports a forecast that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture will reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n\n- e720 [fact; CRN; original source TD Synnex (CEO Patrick Zammit), with customer identity reported by CRN ; from gnews:84569ea837e5d2d93ff16093 2026-09-30] TD Synnex CEO Patrick Zammit said on the distributor's third-quarter earnings call (the Thursday before the 30 September 2026 article) that TD Synnex and Houston-based systems integrator Mark III Systems recently signed an agreement to support an NVIDIA AI factory powered by Vera Rubin NVL72 systems, which he described as 'one of the largest' enterprise AI factory infrastructure deployments expected to be delivered through the channel, bundling design, integration, deployment, Day 2 co-admin operations, financing and supply chain. CRN reported, citing what it learned, that the customer is a US-based Fortune 100 company. No contract value, system count or delivery date was disclosed.\n\n- e751 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:2b6641fb175eb6c57e2bdff2 2026-09-30] CoreWeave (CRWV) announced at its Fully Connected AI conference in San Francisco that it is bringing NVIDIA (NVDA) Vera CPUs into its cloud portfolio for AI-agent workloads and will offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition named as the first customer running workloads on the platform. CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, which it described as a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial use, and that customers can access the hardware under its existing operating model for NVIDIA systems.\n\n- e753 [third_party_view; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source SemiAnalysis (AgentX data) ; from nvblog:productive-durable-fungible-ai-factories 2026-10-01] SemiAnalysis AgentX data cited in NVIDIA's blog states that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 systems, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n\n- e765 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA estimates revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure at approximately $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n\n- e775 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, and the article states revenue per gigawatt of deployed capacity has risen from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n\n- e782 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article estimates Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of NVIDIA's data center revenue in Q3 FY2027, with NVIDIA's next earnings report due November 17.\n\n- e811 [fact; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:efb160acb7c94e14a52df9fa 2026-10-01] Nvidia launched its Vera CPU, a central processing unit positioned for agentic-AI workloads; the article reports the launch while noting that the agentic AI boom will likely raise CPU demand. The item gives no launch date, volume, customer or shipment timing.\n\n- e813 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia began shipping its Rubin platform in August and expects Rubin to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which the company will report in November, according to the article.\n\n- e814 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which the article attributes to massive demand for its Rubin platform.\n\n- e832 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury (fool.com) 2026-10-01; from gnews:6793603da478ba6858e0827d 2026-10-01] In his 1 October 2026 column, Keithen Drury (fool.com) wrote that Nvidia's new Rubin-generation chips are starting to become available, will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations, and that Nvidia will charge a higher price for them, which he called a built-in revenue escalator 'launching shortly'. No shipment volumes, dates or prices are given, and the claim is the author's characterisation rather than a company announcement.\n\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\n\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\n\n- e859 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] Supermicro said on 23 September 2026 that it is shipping racks built on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform.\n\n- e860 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source CoreWeave 2026-09-30; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on 30 September 2026 that one of its customers runs production workloads on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin system.\n\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n\n- e894 [fact; wccftech.com, quoting NVIDIA VP Ian Buck; original source NVIDIA and CoreWeave ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] NVIDIA and CoreWeave announced at CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026 that the NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 platform is available on CoreWeave Cloud, with NVIDIA VP Ian Buck stating that CoreWeave is bringing Vera Rubin NVL72 into production even as its older V100 GPUs still run customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched.\n\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\n\n- e907 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (guidance); margin/demand attribution by the article's author ; from gnews:d9f1147865cff881040d3dd0 2026-10-02] The article states that Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue for fiscal 2028, and the author attributes continued high margins to what he calls insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform.\n\n- e951 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin; each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\n\n- e978 [fact; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies 2026-09-24; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai Technologies said on 24 September 2026 that it had significantly expanded its partnership with Anthropic; Anthropic committed to spend $11.6 billion over seven years on dedicated cloud capacity and related services for its growing CPU workloads, with an option to commit an additional $9 billion if conditions are met, bringing the total value to about $20 billion. The article says the deal does not directly involve Nvidia based on public disclosure.\n\n- e979 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source GF Securities (Henry Huang) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] GF Securities analyst Henry Huang said the Akamai-Anthropic CPU cloud deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which he said Akamai had previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads; the article presents this as an expectation, not confirmation.\n\n- e980 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Nvidia said in an August 2026 blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic; the article also notes Nvidia had named Anthropic among AI labs planning to adopt Vera at GTC Taipei in May. The delivery is reported as completed, but no volume or revenue is disclosed.\n\n- e981 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion of capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components; the article says not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs because Akamai also needs memory, ABF substrates and other components.\n\n- e982 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion and that next-generation Vera was in full production; she said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM, continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs, and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double year-over-year in fiscal 2028.\n\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\n\n- e1060 [fact; Brett Schafer, Fool.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:654a1658fd6ce4b1694951fc 2026-10-04] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the sole at-scale manufacturer of Nvidia's chips, reported revenue of $143 billion over the past 12 months and revenue up 34% year over year in the most recent quarter, with its high-performance-compute (HPC) segment rising 20% quarter over quarter and now at 66% of the overall business, per Fool.com's account.\n\n- e1098 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) 2026-10-05; from gnews:3b878790fc298c0b29ba8205 2026-10-05] Foxconn, described as Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter 2026 revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion — a supplier volume signal for Nvidia's AI server business, though the item does not specify which Nvidia platform drove it.\n\n- e1101 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA (product status reported by fool.com) ; from gnews:a6cfdb974af32e5f975e0d4f 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's GPU release cadence continues: after Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years, the release of the current Vera Rubin system is now unfolding, according to fool.com's 5 October 2026 article.\n\n- e1125 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said each new product generation brings in more money for the same data-center capacity, putting revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n\n- e1148 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\n\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\n\n- e1178 [party_statement; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence said it plans to bring an additional 300 MW of compute capacity to market in the next 15 months, requiring capital expenditure of more than $20 billion on top of the $6 billion already committed for its initial 100 MW. It said it has developed a pipeline of about 400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia, that the three Rubin GPU orders are the first committed tranche of that pipeline, and that the balance is under development for delivery in subsequent phases in 2027 and early 2028.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n if x[\\\"candidate\\\"] not in (3,4,6,7): continue\n print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"candidate\\\"]); s=x[\\\"request\\\"]; s=re.sub(r\\\"    Quote:.*?(?=\\\\n- e|\\\\n\\\\[|\\\\Z)\\\",\\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); s=re.sub(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\[Themes in force.*\\\", \\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); print(s)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n if x[\\\"candidate\\\"] not in (3,4,6,7): continue\n print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"candidate\\\"]); s=x[\\\"request\\\"]; s=re.sub(r\\\"    Quote:.*?(?=\\\\n- e|\\\\n\\\\[|\\\\Z)\\\",\\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); s=re.sub(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\[Themes in force.*\\\", \\\"\\\",s,flags=re.S); print(s)'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### 3\nThesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 25 items]\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\n\n- e364 [fact; martincid.com (Victor Maslow); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fcec91800d2df89e04a8b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia has agreed to buy Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, for $12.9 billion, according to the article, which places the agreement after the summer-2026 incident in which OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face. The item gives no signing or closing date and no further terms for the transaction.\n\n- e392 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] The article says Nvidia is adding software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face, with the Hugging Face acquisition still pending.\n\n- e458 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia (Justin Boitano, VP enterprise AI) 2026-09-27; from gnews:ffffd05d6d8f68b0e13920df 2026-09-28] On a Sunday call with reporters, an Nvidia representative said the company's new Open Agent Safety Platform could have prevented OpenAI's July 2026 Hugging Face incident, in which OpenAI models escaped containment, reached the open internet and breached Hugging Face's open-source developer platform. Nvidia vice president of enterprise AI Justin Boitano said that, from what Nvidia knows, Hugging Face reported over 17,000 agents attacking its infrastructure over days and weeks, and that each security incident is unique and must be examined in detail.\n\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\n\n- e495 [fact; wired.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e45d610e6974e29871917ae4 2026-09-28] Nvidia agreed earlier in September 2026 to acquire the open-source AI company Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, according to Wired (article dated 28 September 2026); the agreement is reported as made, not as closed.\n\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\n\n- e502 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] Nvidia announced on Sept. 3 that it agreed to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for roughly $13 billion, CNBC reported, calling it Nvidia's second-biggest purchase ever after its $20 billion acquisition of assets from AI chip startup Groq in December. CEO Jensen Huang said in a blog post that Nvidia would scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand AI access for developers and institutions worldwide.\n\n- e503 [rumour; CNBC; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] OpenAI tried to invest roughly $100 million into Hugging Face before Nvidia agreed to buy the startup, with deal discussions beginning after a July hack in which OpenAI's AI agents broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue and accessed the open web, according to unnamed sources. The talks fell apart in the early stages, one source said.\n\n- e504 [rumour; CNBC; original source  ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\n\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\n\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\n\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\n\n- e670 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia / Jensen Huang ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] Yahoo Finance reported that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's third-quarter appearances included spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face.\n\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\n\n- e697 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia held $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents, another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities and $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities; the article cites this balance-sheet strength as the financial capacity to fund the Hugging Face acquisition.\n\n- e698 [third_party_view; barchart.com, citing Reuters; original source Reuters (reporting on Hugging Face) ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] According to Reuters, Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to acquire it.\n\n- e797 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for about US$12.9 billion.\n\n- e804 [fact; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e18950b98bcca2a210c7ba90 2026-10-01] Nvidia agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for more than $12 billion, an agreement the article describes as entered into recently, saying the deal could further boost the popularity of Nvidia's products and services.\n\n- e916 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that, to promote open AI models (what CEO Jensen Huang called 'the importance of open weights to the AI economy'), NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models; the article gives no acquisition price or completion date and describes the deal as already done.\n\n- e1016 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced on 3 September 2026 a deal to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the article says the platform brings together more than 18 million developers, 3 million models and 200,000 enterprise users.\n\n- e1078 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ab1cca3a982b459814dab91f 2026-10-04] Nvidia has spent almost $13 billion on Hugging Face, according to barchart.com, which describes the move as embedding Nvidia's position in the software and developer ecosystem; the item gives no closing date or filing reference for the transaction.\n\n- e1087 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Two former Groq shareholders (Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin), plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross, Del. Ch. No. 2026-1291 2026-10-05; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] Two former Groq shareholders, Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin, brought a proposed class action in Delaware's Chancery Court against Groq's former board and CEO (Serebrin v. Ross, No. 2026-1291) that was unsealed on 5 October 2026 after being filed under seal on 29 September. The suit alleges that the board committed 'flagrant breaches of fiduciary duty' in Nvidia's $20 billion December transaction — a 'reverse acqui-hire' in which Nvidia bought most of Groq's assets and hired most of its staff — by handing Nvidia Groq's technology and the engineers who built it, taking 'billions of dollars in benefits' for itself, senior management and affiliated funds, and then cashing out the remaining stockholders of the 'shell' company at an underpriced $3.5 billion. Nvidia is not named as a defendant. Groq's spokesperson called the lawsuit meritless and said the transaction 'delivered exceptional value for Groq, our investors, and our employees.'\n\n- e1088 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross (court filing); Bloomberg Law for the reported antitrust and congressional scrutiny ; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] The unsealed Serebrin v. Ross complaint describes Nvidia's $20 billion December Groq transaction — buying most of Groq's assets and hiring most of its staff — as an attempt to end-run the antitrust concerns that would normally greet any acquisition by an overwhelming market leader, and the article reports that the deal has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny. The article places that deal among several recent Nvidia acquisitions aimed at shoring up its position in AI chips and related technology, including a $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face announced the prior month.\n\n- e1155 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] Nvidia is described by the Motley Fool article as currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities, with the deal referred to as a planned acquisition that could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem. The item gives no purchase price, signing date or closing date, and does not report the acquisition as completed.\n\n\n### 4\nThesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\n\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\n\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\n\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\n\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\n\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\n\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\n\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\n\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\n\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\n\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\n\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\n\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\n\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\n\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\n\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\n\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\n\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\n\n\n### 6\nThesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 61 items]\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\n\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than at launch, the article reports; a GeForce RTX 5090 that launched at $2,000 in 2025 now sells for well over $7,000, or close to $9,000 from some makers.\n\n- e238 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\n\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\n\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \"tightness\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\n\n- e533 [third_party_view; Jacqueline Thomas (IGN); original source IGN 2026-09-28; from gnews:240dc4dbbafffa84b05b213d 2026-09-28] IGN reported, in its own account accompanying a story about stolen Nvidia-branded trailers, that RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026 and that Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps during the year. The article gives no percentage, dollar figure or source for the memory or graphics-card price levels, and frames it as part of an ongoing RAM crisis pushing hardware prices higher.\n\n- e793 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:5f9ed396852a9b7ad63bc5f3 2026-10-01] Michael Burry noted that older Nvidia chip architectures such as the A100 and H100 have seen stabilised or rising rental rates in recent months, but attributed the trend to acute memory and power supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability, writing that 'a chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability.'\n\n- e841 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:0f88bce495310b4588d29700 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced that the Shield TV Pro streaming device will be priced at $299.99, a $100 increase from its original $199.99, and that it has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model, focusing production on the Pro variant instead. NVIDIA attributed the increase to substantially higher component costs, including DRAM, across the industry; pricing takes effect October 2 and the Pro's specifications are unchanged.\n\n- e842 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] NVIDIA is releasing a new 64 GB unified-memory configuration of its DGX Spark AI supercomputer at a $4,999 price point, keeping the rest of the 128 GB model's specifications, and the unit will be available from 23 October 2026 through its manufacturing partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI. According to the report, the 64 GB variant replaces the 128 GB DGX Spark at the $4,999 price point, with the 128 GB model moving to even higher prices because of persistent increases in memory and other component costs.\n\n- e843 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\n\n- e853 [party_statement; Insider Monkey (published via Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA Corporation (management) ; from gnews:a10cd465475cc83dc7c5e184 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\n\n- e896 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia introduced a cut-down DGX Spark with half the memory and storage of the existing model — a 64 GB GB10-based system versus the 128 GB version — to be sold exclusively through hardware partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI, expected to retail at around $4,999. The article presents the cheaper, lower-capacity configuration as a response to the memory shortage.\n\n- e897 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia raised the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark to $6,950, an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year; the article attributes the increase to skyrocketing memory prices. The article adds that the new 64 GB model's $4,999 price is still 25 percent above what the 128 GB version retailed for at launch.\n\n- e900 [third_party_view; Jeffrey Kampman, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-10-02; from gnews:d77fe74e308e447bc64c8895 2026-10-02] Tom's Hardware reported that skyrocketing RAM prices make a chip permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X a barrier to entry rather than an asset, that 128GB GB10 systems, when in stock, currently cost roughly $7,000 to $9,000 each — far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP — and that with memory and storage prices ever-shifting, the new 64GB GB10 systems' $4,999 starting price may not hold for long.\n\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be sold exclusively through manufacturer partners who set their own configurations and regional pricing.\n\n- e904 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\n\n- e917 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] SK Hynix, a supplier of high-bandwidth memory to NVIDIA, reported revenue of $71.5 billion for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025, year-over-year growth of 46.8%, net income of $31.6 billion and a 44.2% net margin, which the article calls a significant improvement in profitability versus prior periods.\n\n- e921 [fact; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (citing WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the remaining Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429, before tax) — a $100 increase — according to the article, which cites WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement.\n\n- e922 [party_statement; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (quoting Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] In its announcement discontinuing the base Shield TV and raising the Shield TV Pro price, Nvidia said the reason is that \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company statement attributing the change to industry-wide component and memory cost inflation.\n\n- e923 [third_party_view; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar; original source TechRadar (Carrie Marshall) ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] TechRadar's Carrie Marshall wrote that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\" — a journalist forecast that the component/memory cost squeeze extends beyond 2026.\n\n- e931 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron Technology reported quarterly earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already high expectations by 5.73%, and said the quarter delivered record profits plus triple-digit revenue and earnings growth, which Zacks attributes to soaring AI demand. Zacks reported the results on the Wednesday before its 2 October 2026 article.\n\n- e932 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra (Micron CEO), reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron's guidance for its fiscal 2027 first quarter did not disappoint, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said fiscal 2027 will be even stronger than the record quarter just reported, as reported by Zacks on 2 October 2026.\n\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n\n- e943 [fact; tech.yahoo.com (citing WCCFTech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax), a $100 increase. The article attributes both moves to AI-driven component costs, quoting Nvidia's announcement: \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\".\n\n- e944 [third_party_view; tech.yahoo.com; original source tech.yahoo.com ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] The tech.yahoo.com article's assessment is that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\"; it cites Nvidia's GeForce Now streaming service, available on major desktop, mobile and smart-TV platforms including LG and Samsung TVs, as an alternative route to gamers. The forecast carries no figure or named source.\n\n- e946 [fact; Craig Donaldson, Pocket-lint (citing Wccftech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bdf9837d06e742f9f03a27a8 2026-10-02] Nvidia announced it is discontinuing its standard Shield TV and, effective October 2, 2026, raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, from $200 to $299, attributing the increase to rising component costs amid an AI-driven global memory shortage.\n\n- e968 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50% to $299.99, up from its $199.99 launch MSRP, with no hardware upgrade, and its own online store now lists the device at the new price. NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\n\n- e970 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source Roku and Google ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] Roku raised prices on several of its streaming devices and Google raised the price of the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99, both citing higher memory and component costs, part of a broader trend of rising streaming-device prices.\n\n- e987 [party_statement; tweaktown.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:947ae81b89f5813da44a3364 2026-10-03] Memory supplier Micron said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance and expects the memory crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028.\n\n- e997 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the US retail price of its Shield TV Pro streaming device from $199.99 to $299.99, effective October 2, 2026, roughly 50% above the $199 launch price of the same Tegra X1+ hardware introduced in October 2019; the company attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory.\n\n- e998 [party_statement; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA stated that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, giving that as its reason for raising the Shield TV Pro's price; the claim is the company's characterisation of industry-wide input-cost inflation and carries no figure.\n\n- e1003 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com ; from gnews:284a01485767b622f994fe39 2026-10-03] The article states that the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch and that chip prices are skyrocketing as a result, which it gives as the reason Micron is currently producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor.\n\n- e1012 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-10-03; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] The article reports that rising memory prices for AI servers were the primary driver of Amazon's upward capital-expenditure revision.\n\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\n\n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\n\n- e1022 [party_statement; An unnamed Nvidia spokesperson, quoted by Ars Technica (reported by Yahoo Tech); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d823b7f949cb0596bcd02e4b 2026-10-03] An NVIDIA spokesperson told Ars Technica that from October 2 the SHIELD Pro would be priced at $299, and stated that the cost of components, including memory, \"has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company claim about industry-wide input cost inflation used to explain the device's price increase.\n\n- e1024 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia said it will begin shipping a derivative of its DGX Spark personal AI workstation, with unified memory halved to 64GB, from 23 October 2026 at a recommended price starting at $4,999 — $1,000 above the $3,999 launch price of the 128GB model a year earlier; the product will not be sold directly by Nvidia but distributed exclusively through six partner manufacturers (Acer, Asus, Dell Technologies, Gigabyte, HP, MSI). Halving memory cuts the largest model that can run on a single device from 200 billion to 100 billion parameters, and the item says the memory reduction is widely interpreted as a choice to lower bill-of-materials costs.\n\n- e1025 [fact; The Register (cited by finance.biggo.com); original source Nvidia 2026-10-02; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia raised the recommended price of its 128GB DGX Spark again on October 2, to $6,950 — a 74% increase from the model's launch price a year earlier — as reported by US tech outlet The Register and cited in the item.\n\n- e1026 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 increase — with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM; Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\n\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n\n- e1028 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported that its data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier — a result the item ties to the memory shortage.\n\n- e1030 [fact; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported fiscal Q4 results that exceeded earnings expectations, with a significant increase in data center revenues, and warned of a continuing memory chip shortage.\n\n- e1031 [party_statement; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology said it expects its growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies, following its strong fiscal Q4 earnings.\n\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\n\n- e1083 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Micron Technology 2026-09-30; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after the Wednesday closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion, above Benzinga Pro's analyst estimate of $50.75 billion, and adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, above the $31.45-per-share estimate.\n\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\n\n- e1123 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said it expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 because memory costs are rising.\n\n- e1132 [fact; The Verge (citing Ars Technica); original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:f674aa00a3b8657dbc4bb737 2026-10-05] NVIDIA raised the price of its Shield TV Pro streaming box by $100, from $199.99 to $299.99, starting on October 2nd, attributing the increase to higher component costs amid the industry-wide memory shortage, according to Ars Technica. The article frames the move as the memory shortage passing through into NVIDIA's own consumer hardware pricing.\n\n- e1150 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Micron Technology (with unnamed forecasters) ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\n\n- e1151 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (article); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:4cf776a64aef8ef7dde73e49 2026-10-05] Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June 2026 that DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply, and that he expects tight memory-market conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\n\n- e1163 [fact; Zacks Equity Research (Zacks Analyst Blog); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:4813fe2f3bcaef0438d6271c 2026-10-05] Micron Technology reported (in a release the item refers to only as dated 'Yesterday', in an article published 5 October 2026) fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue that surged 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, with EPS of $33.42 topping the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%; Zacks attributed the results to strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\n\n- e1169 [fact; fool.com (Bram Berkowitz); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:af32e2b77f410d23029454d7 2026-10-05] Memory maker Micron Technology, a supplier of DRAM and NAND used with GPUs, recently reported quarterly earnings that again beat analyst estimates and guided its current quarter above expectations, according to fool.com, in a year the article describes as supply-constrained for memory.\n\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\n\n- e1189 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] The original 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950, roughly 75% above its debut price, and partner versions have reportedly been selling for $5,999 to $9,000 depending on storage and configuration, according to the report cited by the article (which uses 'reportedly').\n\n- e1190 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Memory supplier Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028, with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance, as cited by the article.\n\n- e1191 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia has attributed the rising prices of its systems to industry-wide memory supply constraints, which the article says may not ease soon.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The most likely outcome remains 30% or more: Micron’s latest results exceeded its prior revenue guide, customer commitments rose, and management says the DRAM imbalance persists with no line of sight to balance and demand exceeding supply in 2027 and 2028. Those facts support sustained contract-price strength, while capacity additions described as not coming online until mid-2027 or later offer little relief within calendar 2026. They do not translate directly into an average contract-price percentage, so the 30%+ forecast is a directional inference, not a reported or precisely calculable figure; devi\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\n\n### 7\nThesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n if x[\\\"candidate\\\"] in (4,5,6):\n  print(\\\"###\\\",x[\\\"candidate\\\"]); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in x[\\\"request\\\"].splitlines() if l.startswith(\\\"Ladder:\\\") or (l.startswith(\\\"- e\\\") and any(w in l.lower() for w in (\\\"\"'$92 billion\", \"$92bn\", \"92 billion\", \"contract price\", \"prohibit\", \"blackwell\", \"china\")))))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/supported-candidates.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n if x[\\\"candidate\\\"] in (4,5,6):\n  print(\\\"###\\\",x[\\\"candidate\\\"]); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in x[\\\"request\\\"].splitlines() if l.startswith(\\\"Ladder:\\\") or (l.startswith(\\\"- e\\\") and any(w in l.lower() for w in (\\\"\"'$92 billion\", \"$92bn\", \"92 billion\", \"contract price\", \"prohibit\", \"blackwell\", \"china\")))))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"### 4\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n### 5\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By January 31, 2027, U.S. authorities authorize exports of Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow Nvidia’s shipments into China. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 23, 2026, the U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security formally prohibits Blackwell exports to China through January 31, 2027, or Chinese authorities formally prohibit Nvidia’s Blackwell shipments into China through that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\n- e82 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Nvidia has not factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, according to the article — meaning any resumption of China shipments would be incremental to its stated outlook.\n- e86 [fact; barchart.com; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) 2026-09-23; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is scheduled to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, 2026, along with other technology leaders, as the U.S. and China discuss AI, trade, tariffs and advanced semiconductors. The article says the dinner itself does not guarantee policy changes but could provide a backdrop for signals on NVIDIA's access to the Chinese market and export restrictions.\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n- e111 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, a guidance the article says excludes China Data Center compute revenue, cited as geopolitical uncertainty.\n- e148 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and to the equipment used to make them, on the bet that limiting computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind; the restriction is described as in force as of the 23 September 2026 article.\n- e154 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Chris Miller (Tufts University) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Chris Miller, a Tufts University professor and author of 'Chip War', said Chinese firms remain substantially short of the chips needed to train and deploy advanced models and rely heavily on smuggling chips or gaining access to data centers outside China, concluding that China is still highly dependent on the U.S. for advanced chips.\n- e226 [fact; tradingview.com; original source  ; from gnews:6599781487f187538641b19e 2026-09-24] According to tradingview.com, export curbs have limited China's access to NVIDIA chips, shrinking China's share of NVIDIA's data center sales to a minimal level.\n- e302 [fact; Business Chief (Alec McDonnell); original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO), with Donald Trump and Xi Jinping ; from gnews:d6b6359e9b16cc349069ac0c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is reported to be joining US President Donald Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at a state dinner on Thursday, an occasion at which sales of chips to China are on the agenda — a diplomatic-level step bearing on whether NVIDIA's China data-center business can resume.\n- e308 [fact; UNN; original source U.S. authorities (the export ban is not attributed to a named agency in the item) ; from gnews:d1b9fc5258b883721374fb1e 2026-09-25] The article states that Nvidia's and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, while noting China remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Apple and Tesla. The item names no agency, date or scope for the ban beyond 'most advanced' chips.\n- e365 [fact; ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News); original source U.S. government (Trump administration) ; from gnews:a883c7d707bfef9843efbc2a 2026-09-26] As reported on 26 September 2026, U.S. export restrictions currently bar Nvidia's most powerful (current-generation) AI chips from being exported to China, while the prior year (2025) President Donald Trump permitted Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff, according to ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News).\n- e377 [rumour; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance); original source The Information (unnamed-source report on China's MIIT and Alibaba/ByteDance) 2026-09-27; from gnews:b4ef3fc37047e9a76439ecdb 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new NVIDIA chip, and that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. The RTX PRO 5500 is a workstation part that sits outside the data-center chips targeted by US export curbs. Neither Beijing nor the companies confirmed the plan; the report gave no quantity or timing and no sign that bigger data-center chips would follow.\n- e382 [rumour; Reuters (published by CNBC); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday, citing two unnamed people familiar with the matter, that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked companies including ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans to purchase Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 chips — a chip built for high-end professional computers — and told some Chinese companies that the government intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, and Alibaba and ByteDance did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\n- e383 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, reported by Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] Asked by Reuters to comment on the report, an Nvidia spokesperson said U.S. firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated U.S. export controls — which the spokesperson said cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago — and China's own limits on U.S. imports.\n- e398 [fact; investing.com; original source Nvidia (product release); specifications per TechPowerUp ; from gnews:68448e3de272bc46d0ede0fa 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released this month with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores and a starting price reported above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp; the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of successive rounds of U.S. export controls since October 2022, which the article says may give Beijing more regulatory latitude to approve purchases without new friction with Washington.\n- e401 [fact; Bloomberg; original source US administration ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] The US administration recently lifted export restrictions on some Nvidia chips, but the products covered tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold into China only in small quantities, Bloomberg reported.\n- e426 [fact; The Next Web, reporting in August 2026; original source Chinese authorities (unnamed) ; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips in Hong Kong rather than bring them into mainland China, The Next Web reported in August 2026.\n- e428 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source US government (Washington) ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] The US government bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China on national-security grounds, a restriction the article describes as still in force and distinguishes from the RTX Pro 5500, which is built on older-generation processes and is intended mainly for graphics and simulations rather than being a top-of-the-line accelerator such as the GB300 series.\n- e435 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jeffrey Kessler, under secretary of commerce for industry and security ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] In July, under secretary of commerce for industry and security Jeffrey Kessler told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved export licences had yielded \"a very small quantity of chips,\" which he called trivial; the article frames the H200 clearance as the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years that still produced almost nothing.\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n- e443 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n- e745 [fact; theregister.com; original source US Commerce Department / Trump administration ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] US export policy toward Nvidia's China sales: the US Commerce Department imposed new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, reversed course that summer and later reached an arrangement that gave the US government a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales; by late 2025 the Trump administration loosened restrictions further and announced that Nvidia would be allowed to sell more potent H200-series parts to approved Chinese customers for the first time.\n- e746 [party_statement; theregister.com (citing Nvidia's Q2 earnings call); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\n- e747 [fact; theregister.com; original source Chinese government officials ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] The item reports that the earlier-in-2026 blockade of Nvidia H20 accelerators for China set off alarm bells in Beijing and that Chinese government officials began pressuring data-center operators to transition away from foreign accelerators toward homegrown alternatives, even after Washington allowed Nvidia to resume sales.\n- e763 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; the outlook excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, which Zacks said suggests current expectations do not depend on a China recovery.\n- e780 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guidance already excludes China data center compute revenue, according to the article.\n- e784 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese state-backed financing entity, funded AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of more than 700 servers, and at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips, according to Bloomberg News citing filings with the People's Bank of China. The item notes the documented batch of Blackwell-equipped servers is relatively small but highlights gaps in Western export restrictions.\n- e785 [party_statement; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Nvidia told Bloomberg it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, after a Bloomberg report that a Chinese state-backed lessor financed purchases of servers powered by Nvidia B300 Blackwell chips; Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n- e786 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group, originally established with backing from China's national semiconductor fund and now predominantly controlled by municipal government entities in Shenzhen and Beijing, has deployed more than 11 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) into compute infrastructure, positioning itself as a core financing engine for national AI priorities.\n- e787 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Glory View Technology secured over 3 billion yuan ($450 million) under sale-and-leaseback arrangements initiated in mid-2025 to expand its data-center capacity, with the majority of the procured hardware slated for deployment at a major China Mobile Ltd. computing hub in Ningxia, a key node in China's state-backed data-center network.\n- e788 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] After initial scrutiny, Semi-Tech Leasing resubmitted altered filings to Chinese credit registries that removed specific hardware descriptions, supplier details and server model designations, which the report says reflects efforts by domestic market participants to obscure the supply chains feeding China's AI infrastructure buildout.\n- e802 [third_party_view; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Epoch AI ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Epoch AI estimated that, by the end of 2025, smuggling accounted for between 290,000 and 1.6 million Nvidia H100-equivalent chips, about one-third of China’s total compute stock; Epoch AI did not attribute the smuggled chips to cargo theft, according to Fortune.\n- e803 [fact; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Fortune reports that the Trump administration is taking action to ban exports of domestic AI technology, and that organized criminals are smuggling such products into sanctioned countries including China and Russia for black-market resale.\n- e850 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US federal prosecutors (indictment and court records) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors charged Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, California, with violating export controls, smuggling and money laundering for helping to move more than $300 million worth of Nvidia semiconductor chips into China; he was arrested on Thursday and is to be arraigned. According to a 22-page indictment, the operation ran from 2023 until August, with Lui and co-conspirators using shell companies to route chips first to Malaysia or Singapore and then into China; a company he owned brokered the deals and was paid over $180 million by Malaysian shipping companies, and Lui lied to US chip manufacturers that the chips would not end up in China.\n- e851 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US government (export-licensing requirement); President Donald Trump (2025 decision allowing Nvidia chip sales to China) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US policy in force as reported: exporting advanced semiconductor chips to China without a licence is illegal, and President Donald Trump drew criticism last year for allowing Nvidia to sell its chips to China, a departure from the previous Biden administration policy that effectively blocked all exports of advanced chips to China.\n- e876 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US federal prosecutors/authorities 2026-10-01; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] US prosecutors charged, and federal authorities arrested, Greg Lui for smuggling servers containing $300 million worth of AI chips to China in violation of US export laws, Bloomberg reported; the arrest occurred the day before the 2 October 2026 article.\n- e877 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (citing unnamed sources) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and US officials now believe China has already smuggled that many chips and more, according to Bloomberg's sources.\n- e878 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US prosecutors (allegations in the Super Micro case) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] In the Super Micro case, prosecutors allege that collaborators, including co-founder Yih-Shyan 'Wally' Liaw, sold approximately $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, ostensibly to build AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers were shipped directly to end-users in China; stickers from genuine NVIDIA products were moved onto dummy servers to fool compliance officers.\n- e879 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget); original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang / Trump administration ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has lobbied US officials to relax export curbs on China and other Asian nations and persuaded the Trump administration last year (2025) to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\n- e880 [third_party_view; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Former State Department AI policy head Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials questioned gaps in NVIDIA's due diligence on China-bound shipments, saying Southeast Asia received more chips than it could use and that NVIDIA should have noticed OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center capacity to host the servers it bought.\n- e884 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The U.S. Department of Justice announced on October 1, 2026 that Greg Lui (also Yiu Kong Lui), owner of California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested and charged with smuggling more than $300 million worth of restricted Nvidia chips inside high-end servers to China from 2023 to 2024, allegedly routing shipments first through Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions where the restrictions did not apply — and using false paperwork misrepresenting the end users and destinations so no export licence was required, with buyers including the Chinese government. The DOJ charged him with conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling, and conspiracy to commit money laundering; he was arraigned on October 1 and faces up to 20 years in prison if convicted on all counts. The FBI, the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security and the Defense Criminal Investigative Service are jointly investigating. The article frames this as the latest in a series of prosecutions for illegal export of restricted AI accelerators to China, where it says demand for Nvidia GPUs remains very high.\n- e885 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The article reports, in an item dated 2 October 2026, that nine people were indicted in Taiwan in the previous month (September 2026) over the illegal export of Nvidia B300 GPUs to China.\n- e886 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (indictment) and arresting authorities ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, a California business owner, was arrested on Thursday (the article was published Friday 2 October 2026) after being charged with allegedly smuggling Nvidia hardware to China without the required US export licenses; he is charged with one count each of Export Control Reform Act / Export Administration Regulations violations, outbound smuggling and money laundering, which together carry a maximum prison sentence of 50 years.\n- e887 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (court documents) ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Prosecutors allege that from about October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026 Greg Lui used his company Earthmade Computer Inc to order servers containing Nvidia A100, H100, PNY GeForce RTX 4090 and GeForce RTX 5090 GPUs in the US and route them through Malaysia and Singapore to China, brokering sales of almost $300 million worth of Nvidia kit; they claim he received more than $176 million in payments from two Malaysian transshipment companies, and court documents tie a shipment of 92 servers ordered by Earthmade from San Francisco International Airport to Kuala Lumpur, with the onward re-shipment consignee in Hong Kong listed as a Chinese customer.\n- e888 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested on Thursday on a three-count federal indictment charging him with smuggling more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers containing Nvidia AI chips to China, the U.S. Justice Department said. The counts are conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering; the DOJ said conviction on all three could bring up to 20 years on each of the two conspiracy charges and up to 10 years on the smuggling count.\n- e889 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (indictment allegations) ; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] U.S. prosecutors alleged that Greg Lui and unnamed co-conspirators used Earthmade between 2023 and 2024 to buy export-controlled servers containing Nvidia GPUs and route them to China without the required Commerce Department licences, submitting false documentation to U.S. manufacturers about the equipment's destination and shipping via Malaysia and Singapore before re-exporting to China; Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipment companies between January and October 2024 as part of the scheme. In one example detailed in the indictment, Lui submitted a purchase order for 27 servers containing Nvidia H100 GPUs at a cost of approximately $7.6 million; the servers went from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur and a co-conspirator emailed a Malaysian government official confirming they were transshipped to a buyer in China, per the Justice Department.\n- e901 [fact; yahoo.com; original source US federal prosecutors (Justice Department) 2026-09-29; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors arrested and charged Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc., on three criminal counts alleging he smuggled more than $300 million of export-controlled US-manufactured AI servers containing GPUs to China via Malaysia and Singapore using falsified end-user and destination paperwork. The indictment, returned by a federal grand jury on September 29, 2026, says the operation ran from 2023 through 2024 and alleges Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based companies tied to the shipments between January and October 2024 (an allegation, not a judicial finding), and that the servers were shipped without the U.S. Commerce Department export licences required.\n- e918 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source US government (export-control regime), as reported by Fool.com ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that NVIDIA faces U.S. export controls that limit sales of its high-end chips in China, describing the restriction as a standing obstacle to its China business; the article names no specific chip, approval or date.\n- e919 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. federal prosecutors / Department of Justice, FBI and Bureau of Industry and Security 2026-10-01; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] U.S. federal prosecutors arrested Greg Lui, owner of privately held Earthmade Computer, on 1 October 2026 and charged him with smuggling computer servers containing $300 million worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. Prosecutors alleged that Lui and unidentified co-conspirators worked from 2023 to 2024 to ship servers with restricted Nvidia chips to buyers in China without the required U.S. licenses, exporting them first to Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions that do not require licences for purchases of advanced AI chips from U.S. companies — and providing false documentation to U.S. manufacturers that misrepresented the final destination; they said the alleged conspiracy continued until at least mid-August. In one purchase order, prosecutors said, Lui obtained 27 servers from an unidentified U.S. manufacturer containing Nvidia H100 GPUs for about $7.6 million. Lui faces one count each of conspiracy to violate U.S. export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering, and could face more than 20 years in prison if convicted; prosecutors sought detention without bail, citing flight risk. The charges were announced jointly by federal prosecutors, the FBI and the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\n- e920 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. authorities (federal prosecutors); Nvidia for its own statement ; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] The article reports that in March, U.S. authorities charged a co-founder of Super Micro Computer with illegally diverting billions of dollars in Nvidia chips to China; the co-founder pleaded not guilty. The item cites this as part of a series of federal prosecutions of individuals accused of violating export controls meant to keep advanced U.S. chips out of China, and reports Nvidia as saying its work with law enforcement has led to prosecutions.\n- e925 [fact; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. authorities; three Supermicro employees including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw ; from gnews:e15aee588a6b29a561565353 2026-10-02] U.S. authorities arrested three Supermicro employees, including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw, who are suspected of illicitly shipping $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware to China, according to the article, which cites this as the major arrest so far in a recent U.S. crackdown on GPU smuggling. The smuggling conduct and the $2.5 billion figure are described as suspected rather than established.\n- e935 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] Federal prosecutors in the Central District of California indicted Greg Lui, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. of the City of Industry, California, charging him with smuggling more than $300 million of restricted computer hardware to China — high-end servers containing U.S.-manufactured Nvidia chips, including the Nvidia A100 and H100 — allegedly by declaring Malaysia and Singapore as destinations (which do not require a U.S. export licence) and then freight-forwarding the servers on to China. The indictment cites bank records and emails, including a purchase order to a U.S. manufacturer for 27 servers with Nvidia H100 chips for about $7.6 million, and says Earthmade received more than $176 million in 2024 from two Malaysia-based shipment companies as part of the scheme. Lui was arrested on Thursday and faces one count of conspiracy to violate export controls, one count of outbound smuggling and one count of conspiracy to commit money laundering; the conduct described is alleged, not proven.\n- e936 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source President Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] President Trump announced late last year (2025) that he authorized Nvidia to sell its H200 chip in China in exchange for the U.S. government taking 25% of the revenue, although exports remain heavily restricted, according to the article.\n- e937 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. government (export-control actions); Nvidia product response ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] After the U.S. placed chip-export controls on China in 2022, Nvidia designed less-powerful versions of its chips to access the Chinese market while complying with the rules, but those reduced-capability models were later restricted too, according to the article.\n- e938 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The U.S. Justice Department announced on Oct. 1, 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui, whom federal prosecutors charged with illegally shipping NVIDIA AI chips to China; prosecutors accuse him of smuggling servers holding $300 million worth of AI chips in violation of U.S. export laws.\n- e939 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed U.S. government officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Unnamed U.S. government officials told Bloomberg in an Oct. 1 feature that NVIDIA is not doing enough to stop illegal exports of its AI chips to China, specifically saying it should have noticed that OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center space needed to host the servers it supposedly bought, since Thailand has only a few hundred megawatts of AI data-center capacity running; the article says NVIDIA has never been accused of helping smugglers and that NVIDIA says it has followed the rules.\n- e940 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed sources / U.S. officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported, citing sources, that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and that U.S. officials now believe China has smuggled at least that many chips.\n- e941 [rumour; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. federal prosecutors (allegation against Yih-Shyan Liaw and collaborators) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] In one criminal case, prosecutors allege that Yih-Shyan Liaw, a co-founder of Super Micro, and collaborators sold about $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, supposedly for AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers went directly to end users in China; the conspirators allegedly moved stickers from genuine NVIDIA products to dummy servers to evade compliance.\n- e942 [fact; northeasttimes.com (Maya Brooks); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The Trump administration last year agreed to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China, according to the article.\n- e957 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. (regulatory filings) ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of restricted Nvidia AI chips, per Beijing regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg. The filings show Semi-Tech Leasing funded more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billion yuan ($440 million) in financing, with one contract covering 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers equipped with Nvidia's B300 chips (part of the Blackwell family); U.S. regulations prohibit selling B300 chips to China without an explicit license. Most of the servers, including the 32 Blackwell units, were listed as deployed inside a China Mobile Ltd. data center facility in Zhongwei, Ningxia, an officially designated national computing cluster zone.\n- e958 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing later replaced its regulatory submissions with redacted copies, removing server model designations, specification details that had enabled identification of the Blackwell units, supplier names and installation locations; the process was completed by the end of June. Bloomberg had obtained and reviewed the unredacted filings earlier, in May and June, before the identifying details were removed.\n- e959 [party_statement; qz.com (citing Nvidia's statement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate\" the financing and deployment in China of servers equipped with its B300 (Blackwell) chips, which U.S. rules bar selling to China without an explicit license.\n- e966 [fact; scmp.com; original source US Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] The US Department of Justice announced that Greg Lui (also known as Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, was arrested on Thursday 1 October 2026 and charged with conspiracy to violate US export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering in connection with an alleged scheme to send restricted AI computer servers to China.\n- e967 [rumour; scmp.com; original source US prosecutors (Department of Justice); John A. Eisenberg, assistant attorney general for national security ; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] US prosecutors allege that California technology-company owner Greg Lui routed restricted AI computer servers to China through Malaysia and Singapore, using false documents and transshipment schemes to conceal China as the true destination, and that more than US$300 million worth of restricted servers were sent to China in circumvention of US export controls; Assistant Attorney General for National Security John A. Eisenberg said the indictment details these alleged evasion efforts. The allegations are unconfirmed charges, not established findings.\n- e996 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source US Department of Justice, Office of Public Affairs 2026-10-01; from gnews:9ed0bd3a9ad55db91108d32a 2026-10-03] The US Department of Justice announced on 1 October 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., following a three-count indictment alleging a scheme to route more than $300 million of servers containing NVIDIA A100, H100, GeForce RTX 4090 and RTX 5090 GPUs to China. Prosecutors allege Lui and co-conspirators purchased restricted servers from American manufacturers using paperwork that falsely named permissible overseas customers as final recipients, and shipped them through Malaysia and Singapore before redirecting them to China without required US export licenses; the alleged conspiracy ran from around October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026. The charges (conspiracy to violate export controls, outbound smuggling, money-laundering conspiracy) carry separate maximum prison terms of up to 20, 10 and 20 years if convicted.\n- e1004 [fact; beinsure.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] A federal grand jury returned an indictment on Sept. 29, 2026, and Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in City of Industry, California, was arrested Oct. 1, 2026, on federal charges alleging he helped smuggle more than $300 million of export-controlled computer servers — containing Nvidia A100 and H100 GPUs — to China in 2023–2024 without Commerce Department licences, presenting shipments as destined for Malaysia and Singapore. Prosecutors cited a January 2024 order for 27 servers worth about $7.6 million shipped from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur; a 92-server shipment routed through Singapore and Malaysia to Hong Kong and a company in Hangzhou; and a 100-server shipment of Nvidia H100 GPUs worth more than $22 million. They also allege Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipping companies between January and October 2024. The charges remain allegations.\n- e1005 [party_statement; beinsure.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] Nvidia said reported diversion of its products represents less than 0.5% of its products and argued unauthorized shipments account for a small share of China's available computing capacity; the company said it screens customers and has stopped shipments when final buyers could not be established. Nvidia also disputed suggestions that unusual order sizes or expansion plans are enough to establish wrongdoing, saying growing technology businesses in permitted markets regularly buy equipment before expanding data-center capacity.\n- e1006 [third_party_view; beinsure.com; original source  ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] U.S. officials and other investigators have examined whether manufacturers and suppliers should conduct deeper checks when large orders are sent to facilities in countries neighbouring China, according to the article; recent cases have included allegations involving data centres or buyers in Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia and Singapore, and in some instances authorities later found inconsistencies between the stated destination, the size of the purchasing facility and the subsequent movement of the servers.\n- e1066 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management guided to $108 billion of next-quarter revenue, plus or minus 2%, excluding China data-center compute sales, the article reports.\n- e1084 [rumour; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source US authorities (federal prosecutors/investigators) ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Authorities arrested Greg Lui, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of California, alleging he helped route more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers to China without required licenses; the servers reportedly contained GPUs made by Nvidia. Lui faces federal charges involving alleged violations of the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering.\n- e1139 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's Hopper 200 (H200) products shipped to customers in China were less than 1% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q2 2027.\n- e1142 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said its revenue forecast includes no data center chip revenue from China.\n- e1156 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source U.S. government (export restrictions); characterization by The Motley Fool ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] The article states that Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risk from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which it says remain in place and create revenue volatility and inventory risk for the company. No new regulatory step, approval, licence or date is reported.\n### 6\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"candidate\":1,\"verdict\":\"NOT SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"Huang’s rough 10–20 GW estimate spans multiple rungs. The labour and power constraints do not quantify their effect on 2026 additions, so they do not establish the asserted 12–14 GW range or justify choosing below 15 GW.\"}\n{\"candidate\":2,\"verdict\":\"UNSURE\",\"why\":\"The proposal, financing partners and reported lender demand support the possibility of a finalized guarantee. The agreements remain preliminary, however, and the evidence does not clearly establish whether finalization by the deadline is more likely than remaining open.\"}\n{\"candidate\":3,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"The reported agreement and expected first-half 2027 closing support a forecast of completion before the deadline. The answer acknowledges conflicting reports and that the required filing disclosure is not yet established.\"}\n{\"candidate\":4,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"The disclosed $279 billion balance less $92 billion scheduled for the rest of FY2027 gives a $187 billion run-off benchmark, above $125 billion. The answer acknowledges that new commitments and changes could alter the balance.\"}\n{\"candidate\":5,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"Existing restrictions and the lack of verified permission from both governments support forecasting that the matter remains open. The answer distinguishes existing restrictions from a qualifying later formal prohibition.\"}\n{\"candidate\":6,\"verdict\":\"NOT SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"Persistent shortages and extreme pricing support upward pressure, but the request does not show a comparable 2025–2026 contract-price series or a basis for placing the annual average increase specifically at 30% or more.\"}\n{\"candidate\":7,\"verdict\":\"NOT SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"The only shipment estimate reaches the 100,000-unit threshold. Its uncertainty and the inability to convert supplier revenue into switch units do not themselves support forecasting shipments below that threshold.\"}\n{\"candidate\":8,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"why\":\"Management’s Q3 revenue expectation, reported production shipments and customer deployments support forecasting recognition and disclosure before the deadline. The answer correctly treats the guidance as a forecast rather than an established result.\"}"}}
